window.HU_ESAT=[{"id": "EA1-1", "paper": "EA1", "n": 1, "q": "<p>A tank is a cuboid with a horizontal rectangular base measuring $2\\text{ m}$ by $1.5\\text{ m}$, and it starts empty. Tap $A$ delivers $15$ litres per minute and tap $B$ delivers $9$ litres per minute. A drain at the bottom removes water at a constant $18$ litres per minute whenever it is open and there is water in the tank.<\/p><p>At the start, tap $A$ is turned on, but the drain has been left open by mistake. Ten minutes later tap $B$ is also turned on. When the water first reaches a depth of $12\\text{ cm}$, the drain is closed. How long after the start does the water reach a depth of $60\\text{ cm}$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$5$ hours $10$ minutes", "$2$ hours $15$ minutes", "$2$ hours $10$ minutes", "$2$ hours $20$ minutes", "$2$ hours", "$1$ hour $15$ minutes"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The base has area $3\\text{ m}^2$, so each centimetre of depth is $0.03\\text{ m}^3 = 30$ litres.<\/p><p>For the first $10$ minutes tap $A$ alone cannot beat the drain ($15 &lt; 18$), so the tank stays empty: the water level cannot go negative. From then on the net rate is $15 + 9 - 18 = 6$ litres per minute, so reaching $12\\text{ cm}$ ($360$ litres) takes $60$ minutes. With the drain closed the rate is $24$ litres per minute, and the remaining $48\\text{ cm}$ ($1440$ litres) takes another $60$ minutes. The total is $10 + 60 + 60 = 130$ minutes.<\/p><p>The trap is to let the tank go to $-30$ litres in the first ten minutes, which adds $5$ minutes and gives $2$ hours $15$ minutes.<\/p>", "tags": ["units", "ratio"], "rating": 6.8}, {"id": "EA1-2", "paper": "EA1", "n": 2, "q": "<p>The times, $t$ minutes, taken by a group of runners to complete a course are shown in a histogram with four bars. The bar for $0\\le t&lt;10$ is $1\\text{ cm}$ high, the bar for $10\\le t&lt;15$ is $3\\text{ cm}$ high, the bar for $15\\le t&lt;20$ is $4\\text{ cm}$ high and the bar for $20\\le t&lt;40$ is $0.75\\text{ cm}$ high. Exactly $30$ runners had times in the interval $10\\le t&lt;15$.<\/p><p>Using linear interpolation, and taking the lower and upper quartiles to be the $\\tfrac{n}{4}$th and $\\tfrac{3n}{4}$th values, where $n$ is the number of runners, estimate the interquartile range in minutes.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{15}{2}$", "$9$", "$\\dfrac{20}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{35}{3}$", "$10$", "$\\dfrac{25}{3}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>In a histogram, frequency is proportional to area, not height. The $10\\le t&lt;15$ bar has area $5\\times 3 = 15$ units for $30$ runners, so each unit of area is $2$ runners. The frequencies are $10\\times 1\\times 2 = 20$, $30$, $5\\times 4\\times 2 = 40$ and $20\\times 0.75\\times 2 = 30$, so $n = 120$.<\/p><p>The cumulative frequencies at $t = 10, 15, 20, 40$ are $20, 50, 90, 120$. The lower quartile (the $30$th value) is $10$ runners into the second class: $10 + 5\\times\\tfrac{10}{30} = \\tfrac{35}{3}$. The upper quartile (the $90$th value) is exactly at $t = 20$. So the interquartile range is $20 - \\tfrac{35}{3} = \\tfrac{25}{3}$.<\/p><p>Taking the bar heights as proportional to frequency, or reading off the lower quartile at a class boundary, gives the popular wrong answers.<\/p>", "tags": ["stats"], "rating": 6.8}, {"id": "EA1-3", "paper": "EA1", "n": 3, "q": "<p>For how many real values of $k$ does the equation<\/p>\\[(k-1)x^2 - 4x + (k+2) = 0\\]<p>have exactly one real solution for $x$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$5$", "$4$", "$1$", "$3$", "$2$", "$6$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>If $k = 1$ the equation is linear, $-4x + 3 = 0$, which has exactly one solution. Otherwise it is a quadratic, with exactly one real solution when the discriminant is zero:<\/p>\\[16 - 4(k-1)(k+2) = 0 \\iff k^2 + k - 6 = 0 \\iff k = -3 \\text{ or } k = 2.\\]<p>So there are $3$ values. The usual answer is $2$, from forgetting that the $x^2$ term can vanish.<\/p>", "tags": ["algebra"], "rating": 6.8}, {"id": "EA1-4", "paper": "EA1", "n": 4, "q": "<p>Find the sum of all real solutions of the equation<\/p>\\[\\left(x^2 - 3x\\right)^2 + \\left(x^2 - 3x\\right) - 6 = 0.\\]", "opts": ["$-3$", "$2$", "$6$", "$3$", "$0$", "$-1$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Put $u = x^2 - 3x$. Then $u^2 + u - 6 = 0$, so $u = 2$ or $u = -3$.<\/p><p>The equation $x^2 - 3x = 2$ has discriminant $9 + 8 = 17 > 0$, so two real roots with sum $3$. The equation $x^2 - 3x + 3 = 0$ has discriminant $9 - 12 &lt; 0$, so no real roots. (Equivalently, $x^2 - 3x = \\left(x - \\tfrac32\\right)^2 - \\tfrac94 \\ge -\\tfrac94$, so $u = -3$ is impossible.)<\/p><p>The sum is $3$. Adding the root sums of both quadratics without checking gives $6$.<\/p>", "tags": ["algebra"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "EA1-5", "paper": "EA1", "n": 5, "q": "<p>Four people are chosen at random. Assume that each person is equally likely to have been born in any of the $12$ months, independently of the others. What is the probability that their four birth months include exactly three different months?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{55}{864}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{55}{144}$", "$\\dfrac{55}{72}$", "$\\dfrac{55}{216}$", "$\\dfrac{41}{96}$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Exactly three months means one pair shares a month and the other two people have different months from each other and from the pair. Choose the pair in $\\binom42 = 6$ ways, then the months for the pair and the two others in $12\\times 11\\times 10$ ways.<\/p>\\[P = \\frac{6\\times 12\\times 11\\times 10}{12^4} = \\frac{7920}{20736} = \\frac{55}{144}.\\]<p>Leaving out the factor $6$ gives $\\tfrac{55}{864}$. The option $\\tfrac{41}{96}$ is the probability that at least two share a month, which also includes three or four sharing and two separate pairs.<\/p>", "tags": ["prob"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "EA1-6", "paper": "EA1", "n": 6, "q": "<p>The numbers $x = 12$ and $y = 4$ are each given correct to the nearest integer. What is the upper bound of<\/p>\\[\\frac{x+y}{x-y}\\,?\\]", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{16}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{15}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{17}{8}$", "$\\dfrac{16}{9}$", "$\\dfrac{17}{7}$", "$2$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Since $x$ and $y$ both appear in the numerator and the denominator, you cannot simply take the largest numerator over the smallest denominator: that would need $x$ at its upper bound and at its lower bound at the same time. Instead write<\/p>\\[\\frac{x+y}{x-y} = 1 + \\frac{2y}{x-y},\\]<p>which increases as $y$ increases and as $x$ decreases. So take $x = 11.5$ and $y = 4.5$, giving $\\dfrac{16}{7}$. The tempting $\\dfrac{17}{7}$ comes from $\\dfrac{12.5+4.5}{11.5-4.5}$, which uses two different values of $x$.<\/p>", "tags": ["number"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "EA1-7", "paper": "EA1", "n": 7, "q": "<p>How many of the following eight numbers are factors of $2^{20} - 1$?<\/p>\\[3,\\quad 5,\\quad 7,\\quad 11,\\quad 13,\\quad 25,\\quad 31,\\quad 41\\]", "opts": ["$3$", "$6$", "$5$", "$8$", "$7$", "$4$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Use the difference of two squares: $2^{20} - 1 = (2^{10} - 1)(2^{10} + 1) = 1023\\times 1025$. Now $1023 = 3\\times 11\\times 31$ and $1025 = 25\\times 41$, so<\/p>\\[2^{20} - 1 = 3\\times 5^2\\times 11\\times 31\\times 41.\\]<p>So $3, 5, 11, 25, 31, 41$ are factors and $7$ and $13$ are not: $6$ in total. Splitting further as $(2^5-1)(2^5+1)(2^{10}+1)$ gives the same factors; it is easy to forget that $25$, not just $5$, divides $1025$.<\/p>", "tags": ["number", "algebra"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "EA1-8", "paper": "EA1", "n": 8, "q": "<p>Priya cycles from $P$ to $Q$ and Quentin cycles from $Q$ to $P$ along the same road. They set off at the same time and each rides at a constant speed. They pass each other after $2$ hours, and Priya reaches $Q$ exactly $1$ hour $20$ minutes after they pass. How long does Quentin's whole journey take?<\/p>", "opts": ["$6$ hours", "$4$ hours $40$ minutes", "$3$ hours $20$ minutes", "$3$ hours", "$4$ hours", "$5$ hours"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Let the speeds be $p$ and $q$. After they pass, Priya still has to cover the stretch Quentin rode in the first $2$ hours, namely $2q$, and she does it in $\\tfrac43$ hours: $2q = \\tfrac43 p$, so $q = \\tfrac23 p$.<\/p><p>Quentin must similarly cover Priya's first stretch $2p$ at speed $q$, which takes $\\dfrac{2p}{q} = 3$ hours. His whole journey is $2 + 3 = 5$ hours. (In general the meeting time is the geometric mean of the two times after meeting: $2^2 = \\tfrac43\\times 3$.) The option $3$ hours stops at the time after passing; $3$ hours $20$ minutes is Priya's journey.<\/p>", "tags": ["ratio", "algebra"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "EA1-9", "paper": "EA1", "n": 9, "q": "<p>$AB$ is a diameter of a circle, and the tangent to the circle at $B$ is drawn. A straight line through $A$ meets the circle again at $C$ and meets the tangent at $T$, with $C$ between $A$ and $T$. Given that $AC = 9$ and $CT = 16$, find the distance from $C$ to the line $AB$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$12$", "$\\dfrac{48}{5}$", "$\\dfrac{36}{5}$", "$\\dfrac{15}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{27}{5}$", "$9$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The tangent is perpendicular to the diameter, so $\\angle ABT = 90^\\circ$, and the angle in a semicircle gives $\\angle ACB = 90^\\circ$. So $BC$ is the altitude from the right angle of triangle $ABT$, whose hypotenuse is $AT = 25$.<\/p><p>Triangles $ACB$ and $ABT$ are similar (common angle $A$, each with a right angle), so $AB^2 = AC\\times AT = 225$ and $AB = 15$; then $BC = \\sqrt{225 - 81} = 12$. In the right-angled triangle $ACB$, the distance from $C$ to $AB$ is the altitude to the hypotenuse:<\/p>\\[h = \\frac{AC\\times BC}{AB} = \\frac{9\\times 12}{15} = \\frac{36}{5}.\\]<p>The options $\\tfrac{27}{5}$ and $\\tfrac{48}{5}$ are the two pieces into which this altitude cuts $AB$.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "EA1-10", "paper": "EA1", "n": 10, "q": "<p>A regular hexagon has area $60\\text{ cm}^2$. Label its sides $1$ to $6$ in order around the hexagon. The midpoints of sides $1$, $3$ and $5$ are joined to form a triangle. What is the area of this triangle in $\\text{cm}^2$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$15$", "$22.5$", "$20$", "$24$", "$30$", "$25$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Take the hexagon with side $2$, made of six equilateral triangles of side $2$, so its area is $6\\sqrt3$. The midpoint of each side is at distance $\\sqrt3$ from the centre (the height of one of those equilateral triangles), and the three chosen midpoints are $120^\\circ$ apart.<\/p><p>So the triangle is equilateral with circumradius $\\sqrt3$, hence side $\\sqrt3\\times\\sqrt3 = 3$ and area $\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{4}\\times 9 = \\tfrac{9\\sqrt3}{4}$. The ratio to the hexagon is $\\tfrac{9}{24} = \\tfrac38$, and $\\tfrac38\\times 60 = 22.5$. The option $30$ is the triangle on alternate vertices, which is half the hexagon.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "EA1-11", "paper": "EA1", "n": 11, "q": "<p>What is the smallest positive integer $n$ such that $12n$ is a perfect square and $18n$ is a perfect cube?<\/p>", "opts": ["$36$", "$144$", "$24$", "$72$", "$12$", "$6$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Any prime other than $2$ or $3$ in $n$ would only make $n$ bigger, so let $n = 2^a 3^b$. Then $12n = 2^{a+2}3^{b+1}$ is a square when $a$ is even and $b$ is odd, and $18n = 2^{a+1}3^{b+2}$ is a cube when $a\\equiv 2$ and $b\\equiv 1 \\pmod 3$.<\/p><p>The smallest choices are $a = 2$ and $b = 1$, so $n = 12$. Check: $12\\times 12 = 144 = 12^2$ and $18\\times 12 = 216 = 6^3$.<\/p>", "tags": ["number"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "EA1-12", "paper": "EA1", "n": 12, "q": "<p>Evaluate<\/p>\\[\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1}+1\\sqrt{2}} + \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}+2\\sqrt{3}} + \\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt{4}} + \\cdots + \\frac{1}{100\\sqrt{99}+99\\sqrt{100}}.\\]", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{11}{10}$", "$1$", "$\\dfrac{10}{11}$", "$9$", "$\\dfrac{99}{100}$", "$\\dfrac{9}{10}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>The general term is $\\dfrac{1}{(k+1)\\sqrt{k} + k\\sqrt{k+1}}$. Take out $\\sqrt{k}\\sqrt{k+1}$ and rationalise:<\/p>\\[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{k}\\sqrt{k+1}\\left(\\sqrt{k+1}+\\sqrt{k}\\right)} = \\frac{\\sqrt{k+1}-\\sqrt{k}}{\\sqrt{k}\\sqrt{k+1}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{k}} - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{k+1}}.\\]<p>Summing from $k = 1$ to $99$ telescopes to $1 - \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{100}} = \\dfrac{9}{10}$. Writing $\\tfrac1k - \\tfrac1{k+1}$ by mistake gives $\\tfrac{99}{100}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["number", "algebra"], "rating": 7.1}, {"id": "EA1-13", "paper": "EA1", "n": 13, "q": "<p>The positive real number $x$ satisfies $x - \\dfrac{1}{x} = 3$. Find the value of<\/p>\\[x^6 - \\frac{1}{x^6}.\\]", "opts": ["$396$", "$120\\sqrt{13}$", "$36\\sqrt{13}$", "$360\\sqrt{13}$", "$-360\\sqrt{13}$", "$1296$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Factorise: $x^6 - \\dfrac{1}{x^6} = \\left(x^3 - \\dfrac{1}{x^3}\\right)\\left(x^3 + \\dfrac{1}{x^3}\\right)$.<\/p><p>With $t = x - \\tfrac1x = 3$, cubing gives $x^3 - \\tfrac1{x^3} = t^3 + 3t = 36$. Next, $\\left(x + \\tfrac1x\\right)^2 = t^2 + 4 = 13$, and since $x>0$, $s = x + \\tfrac1x = \\sqrt{13}$. Then $x^3 + \\tfrac1{x^3} = s^3 - 3s = 13\\sqrt{13} - 3\\sqrt{13} = 10\\sqrt{13}$.<\/p><p>So the answer is $36\\times 10\\sqrt{13} = 360\\sqrt{13}$. Ignoring the condition $x>0$ allows the negative value; using $x^3 + \\tfrac1{x^3} = 36$ as well gives $1296$.<\/p>", "tags": ["algebra"], "rating": 7.1}, {"id": "EA1-14", "paper": "EA1", "n": 14, "q": "<p>A triangle has sides of length $5$, $7$ and $8$. What is the radius of the circle inscribed in the triangle?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$", "$\\sqrt{3}$", "$\\dfrac{5\\sqrt{3}}{4}$", "$2$", "$\\dfrac{7\\sqrt{3}}{3}$", "$2\\sqrt{3}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>By the cosine rule, the angle $\\theta$ opposite the side $7$ has $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{25 + 64 - 49}{2\\times 5\\times 8} = \\dfrac12$, so $\\theta = 60^\\circ$ and the area is $\\tfrac12\\times 5\\times 8\\times\\sin 60^\\circ = 10\\sqrt3$.<\/p><p>Splitting the triangle into three triangles from the incentre gives area $= r\\times s$, where $s = 10$ is the semi-perimeter. So $r = \\dfrac{10\\sqrt3}{10} = \\sqrt3$. Using the full perimeter gives $\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}$; the option $\\tfrac{7\\sqrt3}{3}$ is the circumradius.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 7.1}, {"id": "EA1-15", "paper": "EA1", "n": 15, "q": "<p>The five numbers $3$, $7$, $12$, $15$ and $x$, where $x$ is a real number, have a mean equal to their median. What is the sum of all possible values of $x$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$21$", "$30$", "$23$", "$\\dfrac{37}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{121}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{129}{4}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>The mean is $\\dfrac{37 + x}{5}$. The median depends on where $x$ sits.<\/p><p>If $x\\le 7$ the median is $7$, so $37 + x = 35$ and $x = -2$, which is valid. If $7\\le x\\le 12$ the median is $x$, so $37 + x = 5x$ and $x = \\tfrac{37}{4}$, valid. If $x\\ge 12$ the median is $12$, so $x = 23$, valid.<\/p><p>The sum is $-2 + \\tfrac{37}{4} + 23 = \\tfrac{121}{4}$. Assuming $x$ must be positive (it need not be) loses $-2$ and gives $\\tfrac{129}{4}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["stats"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "EA1-16", "paper": "EA1", "n": 16, "q": "<p>A solid wooden cube has edges of length $10\\text{ cm}$. Three square tunnels, each with cross-section $4\\text{ cm}$ by $4\\text{ cm}$, are cut right through the cube. Each tunnel runs between the centres of a pair of opposite faces, with its sides parallel to the edges of the cube. What is the total surface area, inside and outside, of the resulting solid, in $\\text{cm}^2$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$888$", "$984$", "$1080$", "$600$", "$696$", "$792$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Outside: each face loses a $4\\times 4$ square, leaving $6\\times(100 - 16) = 504$.<\/p><p>Inside: the three tunnels share a central $4\\times 4\\times 4$ cube, all of whose faces are open, so there are no walls there. From each face, a tunnel arm runs $\\tfrac{10 - 4}{2} = 3\\text{ cm}$ in to the central cube, and has four walls of $4\\times 3$, so area $48$. There are $6$ arms, giving $288$.<\/p><p>The total is $504 + 288 = 792$. Treating each tunnel as having full length walls ($3\\times 4\\times 40 = 480$) gives $984$, which counts walls through the hollow centre.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 7.3}, {"id": "EA1-17", "paper": "EA1", "n": 17, "q": "<p>A bag contains $4$ red and $3$ blue counters. Counters are taken out one at a time, at random and without replacement, until the bag is empty. What is the probability that the second blue counter is taken out before the third red counter?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{22}{35}$", "$\\dfrac{3}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{13}{35}$", "$\\dfrac{4}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{18}{35}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>The second blue comes before the third red exactly when at least two of the first four counters are blue (in the first four draws, one of these two events must happen, and they cannot both).<\/p><p>Every set of four counters is equally likely to be the first four, out of $\\binom74 = 35$. All four red: $1$ way. Exactly one blue: $3\\times\\binom43 = 12$ ways. So the probability is $1 - \\tfrac{13}{35} = \\tfrac{22}{35}$.<\/p><p>The option $\\tfrac{13}{35}$ is the complement, and $\\tfrac{18}{35}$ counts only exactly two blues among the first four.<\/p>", "tags": ["prob"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "EA1-18", "paper": "EA1", "n": 18, "q": "<p>Three different vertices of a cube are chosen at random, with every set of three vertices equally likely. What is the probability that they form a right-angled triangle?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{3}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{5}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{4}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{6}{7}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>There are $\\binom83 = 56$ sets, and no three vertices of a cube are collinear. It is easier to count the triangles that are <em>not<\/em> right-angled.<\/p><p>Take a vertex $V$ and its three neighbours: the neighbours are joined to each other by face diagonals, so they form an equilateral triangle. This gives $8$ equilateral triangles, one for each vertex. Every other triangle contains an edge of the cube, and its sides are either $1, 1, \\sqrt2$ (half a face) or $1, \\sqrt2, \\sqrt3$ (an edge, a face diagonal and a space diagonal), taking the edge as $1$. Both satisfy Pythagoras, so all of these are right-angled. So the probability is $\\dfrac{56 - 8}{56} = \\dfrac67$.<\/p><p>Counting only the triangles lying in a face, $6\\times 4 = 24$, gives the trap answer $\\tfrac37$.<\/p>", "tags": ["prob", "geometry"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "EA1-19", "paper": "EA1", "n": 19, "q": "<p>Two chords $AC$ and $BD$ of a circle are perpendicular and meet at a point $X$ inside the circle. Given that $AX = 2$, $XC = 6$ and $BX = 3$, find the radius of the circle.<\/p>", "opts": ["$2\\sqrt{5}$", "$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{65}}{2}$", "$4$", "$\\dfrac{7}{2}$", "$\\sqrt{65}$", "$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Triangles $AXB$ and $DXC$ are similar (angles in the same segment), so $AX\\times XC = BX\\times XD$, giving $XD = \\tfrac{12}{3} = 4$.<\/p><p>Now use coordinates with $X$ at the origin: $A = (-2, 0)$, $C = (6, 0)$, $B = (0, 3)$, $D = (0, -4)$. The centre lies on the perpendicular bisector of $AC$, the line $x = 2$, and on that of $BD$, the line $y = -\\tfrac12$. So the centre is $\\left(2, -\\tfrac12\\right)$ and<\/p>\\[r^2 = (6-2)^2 + \\left(\\tfrac12\\right)^2 = \\tfrac{65}{4}, \\qquad r = \\tfrac{\\sqrt{65}}{2}.\\]<p>This is just over $4$, which is the tempting answer if you assume $AC$ is a diameter.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 7.5}, {"id": "EA1-20", "paper": "EA1", "n": 20, "q": "<p>A sealed hollow cone of height $12\\text{ cm}$ contains some water. When the cone stands on its circular base, with its vertex pointing up, the water is $6\\text{ cm}$ deep. The cone is turned over so that it balances with its vertex pointing down and its base horizontal. How deep, in cm, is the water now? (Ignore the thickness of the walls.)<\/p>", "opts": ["$3\\sqrt[3]{7}$", "$6\\sqrt[3]{7}$", "$6$", "$3\\sqrt{14}$", "$6\\sqrt[3]{4}$", "$10.5$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>With the vertex up, the air fills a small cone of height $6$, which is similar to the whole cone with scale factor $\\tfrac12$, so it has $\\tfrac18$ of the volume. The water is therefore $\\tfrac78$ of the cone.<\/p><p>With the vertex down, the water forms a cone of depth $d$ similar to the whole cone, so $\\left(\\dfrac{d}{12}\\right)^3 = \\dfrac78$ and $d = 12\\times\\dfrac{\\sqrt[3]{7}}{2} = 6\\sqrt[3]{7}\\approx 11.5$. Thinking the water fills half the volume gives $6\\sqrt[3]{4}$; using the scale factor linearly gives $10.5$.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 7.5}, {"id": "EA1-21", "paper": "EA1", "n": 21, "q": "<p>An escalator moves downwards at a constant speed. Ria walks down it, from top to bottom, while it is moving. When she walks at $1$ step per second she takes $30$ steps to reach the bottom. When she walks at $3$ steps per second she takes $45$ steps. How many steps would she take to walk down the escalator if it were switched off?<\/p>", "opts": ["$45$", "$90$", "$60$", "$75$", "$50$", "$40$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Let the escalator have $N$ visible steps and move at $e$ steps per second. At $1$ step per second Ria walks for $30$ seconds, while the escalator carries her $30e$ steps, so $N = 30 + 30e$. At $3$ steps per second she walks for $15$ seconds, so $N = 45 + 15e$.<\/p><p>Equating, $15e = 15$, so $e = 1$ and $N = 60$. The common error is to treat the two trips as taking the same time.<\/p>", "tags": ["ratio", "algebra"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "EA1-22", "paper": "EA1", "n": 22, "q": "<p>How many ordered pairs $(x, y)$ of integers satisfy<\/p>\\[xy = 4x + 6y\\,?\\]", "opts": ["$4$", "$8$", "$12$", "$15$", "$24$", "$16$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Rearrange and factorise: $xy - 4x - 6y + 24 = 24$, so<\/p>\\[(x - 6)(y - 4) = 24.\\]<p>Every way of writing $24$ as a product of two integers $d\\times \\tfrac{24}{d}$ gives exactly one pair, with $x = 6 + d$. The number $24$ has $8$ positive divisors, and $d$ may also be negative, so there are $16$ pairs (including $(0,0)$). Counting only positive divisors gives $8$.<\/p>", "tags": ["algebra", "number"], "rating": 7.7}, {"id": "EA1-23", "paper": "EA1", "n": 23, "q": "<p>Asha and Bilal take turns to roll a fair six-sided die, with Asha going first. Asha wins if she rolls a $6$ on her turn; Bilal wins if he rolls a $5$ or a $6$ on his turn. They keep going until someone wins. What is the probability that Asha wins?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{1}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{5}{8}$", "$\\dfrac{3}{8}$", "$\\dfrac{6}{11}$", "$\\dfrac{3}{11}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{2}$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Let $p$ be the probability that Asha wins. A full round with no winner happens with probability $\\tfrac56\\times\\tfrac46 = \\tfrac59$, after which the game is in its starting position again. So<\/p>\\[p = \\frac16 + \\frac59 p \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\frac49 p = \\frac16 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad p = \\frac38.\\]<p>Going first does not make up for Bilal's doubled chance on each turn. The option $\\tfrac{6}{11}$ is the answer when both need a $6$; $\\tfrac13$ is Asha's share of the single roll chances $\\tfrac16 : \\tfrac26$, which ignores the turn order.<\/p>", "tags": ["prob"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "EA1-24", "paper": "EA1", "n": 24, "q": "<p>Triangle $ABC$ has $AB = 13$, $BC = 14$ and $CA = 15$. The bisector of angle $BAC$ meets $BC$ at $E$, and $M$ is the midpoint of $BC$. What is the area of triangle $AEM$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$3$", "$6$", "$\\dfrac{3}{2}$", "$12$", "$\\dfrac{9}{2}$", "$9$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Drop the perpendicular $AD$ to $BC$ and let $BD = p$. Then $13^2 - p^2 = 15^2 - (14-p)^2$, giving $p = 5$ and $AD = 12$.<\/p><p>The angle bisector theorem gives $BE : EC = AB : AC = 13 : 15$, so $BE = \\tfrac{13}{28}\\times 14 = 6.5$, while $BM = 7$. Hence $EM = 0.5$, and triangle $AEM$ has base $EM$ on $BC$ and height $AD$:<\/p>\\[\\text{area} = \\tfrac12\\times 0.5\\times 12 = 3.\\]<p>The option $9$ is the area of triangle $ADE$ (with $DE = 1.5$), and $12$ is the area of triangle $ADM$ (with $DM = 2$).<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 7.9}, {"id": "EA1-25", "paper": "EA1", "n": 25, "q": "<p>$ABCD$ is a square of side $12$, labelled in order. $E$ is the midpoint of $BC$ and $F$ is the midpoint of $CD$. The line segments $AE$ and $BF$ meet at $P$. What is the area of the quadrilateral $APFD$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$76$", "$72$", "$84$", "$\\dfrac{384}{5}$", "$\\dfrac{432}{5}$", "$\\dfrac{396}{5}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>$APFD$ is what is left of the square after removing the union of triangles $ABE$ and $BCF$. Each has area $\\tfrac12\\times 12\\times 6 = 36$, and they overlap in triangle $BPE$.<\/p><p>Triangles $ABE$ and $BCF$ are congruent, and rotating one onto the other by $90^\\circ$ shows $AE\\perp BF$. So $BPE$ is right-angled at $P$ and similar to $ABE$ (which is right-angled at $B$), with scale factor $\\dfrac{BE}{AE} = \\dfrac{6}{6\\sqrt5} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt5}$. Its area is $\\dfrac{36}{5}$.<\/p>\\[\\text{area of } APFD = 144 - \\left(36 + 36 - \\frac{36}{5}\\right) = 72 + \\frac{36}{5} = \\frac{396}{5}.\\]<p>Forgetting the overlap gives $72$; adding it twice gives $\\tfrac{432}{5}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "EA1-26", "paper": "EA1", "n": 26, "q": "<p>A right-angled triangle has hypotenuse of length $10$, and the circle inscribed in it has radius $1$. What is the area of the triangle?<\/p>", "opts": ["$11$", "$\\dfrac{21}{2}$", "$22$", "$10$", "$12$", "$24$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Let the legs be $a$ and $b$. The incircle touches the two legs at distance $r = 1$ from the right-angled vertex (the centre and the two touching points form a square), and the two tangents from each other vertex are equal. So the hypotenuse is $(a - 1) + (b - 1)$, giving $a + b = 12$.<\/p><p>Then $2ab = (a+b)^2 - (a^2 + b^2) = 144 - 100 = 44$, so the area is $\\tfrac12 ab = 11$. (The legs are $6\\pm\\sqrt{14}$, so such a triangle exists.) Guessing the familiar $6, 8, 10$ triangle gives $24$, but that triangle has inradius $2$.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 8.2}, {"id": "EA1-27", "paper": "EA1", "n": 27, "q": "<p>Two circles, of radii $4$ and $9$, touch each other externally, and both touch a straight line $\\ell$, on the same side of it. A third circle, smaller than both, fits in the gap enclosed by the two circles and $\\ell$: it touches $\\ell$ and touches each of the other two circles externally. What is the radius of the third circle?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{36}{13}$", "$1$", "$\\dfrac{13}{9}$", "$\\dfrac{36}{25}$", "$\\dfrac{3}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{6}{5}$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>First find the distance between the points where two touching circles of radii $R$ and $r$ meet a common line. Their centres are $R + r$ apart and at heights $R$ and $r$, so by Pythagoras the horizontal distance is $\\sqrt{(R+r)^2 - (R-r)^2} = 2\\sqrt{Rr}$.<\/p><p>The touching points of the circles of radii $4$ and $9$ are $2\\sqrt{36} = 12$ apart. If the small circle has radius $x$, its touching point lies between them, $2\\sqrt{4x} = 4\\sqrt{x}$ from the first and $2\\sqrt{9x} = 6\\sqrt{x}$ from the second. So<\/p>\\[4\\sqrt{x} + 6\\sqrt{x} = 12 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\sqrt{x} = \\frac65 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x = \\frac{36}{25}.\\]<p>Stopping at $\\sqrt{x}$ gives $\\tfrac65$; the option $\\tfrac{36}{13} = \\tfrac{4\\times 9}{4+9}$ is a guess at a harmonic mean.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 8.6}, {"id": "EA2-1", "paper": "EA2", "n": 1, "q": "<p>What is the coefficient of $x^5$ in the expansion of $(1 + x + x^2)^6(1 - x)^4$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$0$", "$6$", "$-12$", "$-4$", "$12$", "$-8$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Since $(1 + x + x^2)(1 - x) = 1 - x^3$, the expression is $(1 - x^3)^4(1 + x + x^2)^2 = \\left(1 - 4x^3 + 6x^6 - \\dots\\right)\\left(1 + 2x + 3x^2 + 2x^3 + x^4\\right)$. The second bracket has no $x^5$ term, so the only contribution is $(-4x^3)(3x^2)$, giving $-12$. Taking the $x^2$ coefficient of $(1 + x + x^2)^2$ to be $2$ gives $-8$, and forgetting the square altogether gives $-4$.<\/p>", "tags": ["seq"], "rating": 6.8}, {"id": "EA2-2", "paper": "EA2", "n": 2, "q": "<p>How many integers $x$ satisfy \\[\\dfrac{x^2 - 5x + 4}{x^2 - 9} \\le \\dfrac{1}{2}\\,?\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$4$", "$11$", "$6$", "$5$", "$10$", "$9$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Do not multiply by $x^2 - 9$, whose sign varies. Subtracting $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ gives $\\dfrac{x^2 - 10x + 17}{2(x-3)(x+3)} \\le 0$. The numerator has roots $5 \\pm 2\\sqrt{2}$, roughly $2.17$ and $7.83$. For $-3 &lt; x &lt; 3$ the denominator is negative, so we need the numerator $\\ge 0$: $-3 &lt; x \\le 5 - 2\\sqrt{2}$. For $x > 3$ we need the numerator $\\le 0$: $3 &lt; x \\le 5 + 2\\sqrt{2}$. For $x &lt; -3$ both are positive. The integers are $-2, -1, 0, 1, 2$ and $4, 5, 6, 7$, nine in all. Multiplying through gives $5 - 2\\sqrt{2} \\le x \\le 5 + 2\\sqrt{2}$, which leads to $5$ (or $4$ after removing $x = 3$).<\/p>", "tags": ["alg"], "rating": 6.8}, {"id": "EA2-3", "paper": "EA2", "n": 3, "q": "<p>Water is poured at a constant rate into an empty container in the shape of an inverted right circular cone, with its vertex at the bottom and its axis vertical. When the depth of the water is $4\\text{ cm}$, the depth is increasing at $9\\text{ cm}$ per minute.<\/p><p>How long does it take for the depth to increase from $4\\text{ cm}$ to $8\\text{ cm}$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{28}{27}\\text{ minutes}$", "$\\dfrac{7}{9}\\text{ minutes}$", "$\\dfrac{2}{3}\\text{ minutes}$", "$\\dfrac{4}{9}\\text{ minutes}$", "$\\dfrac{32}{45}\\text{ minutes}$", "$\\dfrac{16}{9}\\text{ minutes}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>By similarity the volume at depth $h$ is $V = ah^3$ for a constant $a$. The constant rate is $\\dfrac{dV}{dt} = 3ah^2\\dfrac{dh}{dt} = 3a(16)(9) = 432a$ per minute. The volume needed is $a(8^3 - 4^3) = 448a$, so the time is $\\dfrac{448}{432} = \\dfrac{28}{27}$ minutes. Assuming the level keeps rising at $9\\text{ cm}$ per minute gives $\\dfrac{4}{9}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff"], "rating": 6.8}, {"id": "EA2-4", "paper": "EA2", "n": 4, "q": "<p>A ball is dropped from rest from a height of $5\\text{ m}$ above a horizontal floor and first hits the floor after $1$ second. Each time it bounces, it rises to $\\dfrac{9}{16}$ of the height from which it last fell. The time taken to fall from rest through a height $h$ is proportional to $\\sqrt{h}$, and the time taken to rise to a height $h$ equals the time taken to fall from that height.<\/p><p>Ignoring the time spent in contact with the floor, what is the average speed of the ball from the moment it is dropped until it comes to rest?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{125}{28}\\text{ m s}^{-1}$", "$\\dfrac{80}{49}\\text{ m s}^{-1}$", "$\\dfrac{20}{7}\\text{ m s}^{-1}$", "$\\dfrac{125}{49}\\text{ m s}^{-1}$", "$5\\text{ m s}^{-1}$", "$\\dfrac{25}{7}\\text{ m s}^{-1}$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Distance: $5 + 2\\left(5\\cdot\\dfrac{9}{16} + 5\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac{9}{16}\\right)^2 + \\dots\\right) = 5 + 2\\cdot\\dfrac{45/16}{7/16} = 5 + \\dfrac{90}{7} = \\dfrac{125}{7}\\text{ m}$. Heights scale by $\\dfrac{9}{16}$, so times scale by $\\sqrt{9/16} = \\dfrac{3}{4}$, and the time is $1 + 2\\left(\\dfrac{3}{4} + \\dfrac{9}{16} + \\dots\\right) = 1 + 2\\cdot\\dfrac{3/4}{1/4} = 7\\text{ s}$. The average speed is $\\dfrac{125}{49}\\text{ m s}^{-1}$. Using the ratio $\\dfrac{9}{16}$ for the times as well gives a time of $\\dfrac{25}{7}\\text{ s}$ and the tempting answer $5$.<\/p>", "tags": ["seq"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "EA2-5", "paper": "EA2", "n": 5, "q": "<p>Which of the following curves can be obtained from the curve $y = \\log_2 x$ by a single transformation, where the transformation is either one translation or one stretch parallel to a coordinate axis (with the other axis fixed and a positive scale factor)?<\/p><ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$y = \\log_2 (8x)$<\/li><li>$y = 3 + \\log_8 x$<\/li><li>$y = \\log_2 (x^2)$<\/li><\/ol>", "opts": ["II only", "III only", "II and III only", "I and II only", "none of them", "I only", "I, II and III", "I and III only"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>I: $\\log_2(8x) = 3 + \\log_2 x$, a translation of $3$ units upwards (it is also a stretch parallel to the $x$-axis with factor $\\dfrac{1}{8}$). II: $y = 3 + \\dfrac{1}{3}\\log_2 x$. A stretch parallel to the $y$-axis keeps the point $(1,0)$, but II passes through $(1,3)$. A translation or a stretch parallel to the $x$-axis gives $y = \\log_2(x-a) + b$, with gradient $\\dfrac{1}{(x-a)\\ln 2}$, which cannot equal the gradient $\\dfrac{1}{3x\\ln 2}$ of II for every $x$. So II needs two transformations. III: $\\log_2(x^2) = 2\\log_2|x|$ is defined for every $x \\neq 0$ and has two branches, while any translation or stretch of $y = \\log_2 x$ is defined only on a half-line. It agrees with the stretch $y = 2\\log_2 x$ only for $x > 0$. So only I works.<\/p>", "tags": ["explog", "graphs"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "EA2-6", "paper": "EA2", "n": 6, "q": "<p>What is the sum of all real solutions of the equation \\[\\log_{x-1}(x^2 - 5x + 7) + 2\\log_{x^2 - 5x + 7}(x - 1) = 3\\,?\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$2$", "$8$", "$4$", "$6$", "$11$", "There are no real solutions."], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Both bases must be positive and not equal to $1$. As $x^2 - 5x + 7 = \\left(x - \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)^2 + \\dfrac{3}{4} > 0$, this means $x > 1$, $x \\neq 2$ and $x \\neq 3$. Writing $L = \\log_{x-1}(x^2 - 5x + 7)$, the second term is $\\dfrac{2}{L}$, so $L^2 - 3L + 2 = 0$ and $L = 1$ or $L = 2$. $L = 1$ gives $x^2 - 5x + 7 = x - 1$, so $x = 2$ or $x = 4$, and only $x = 4$ is allowed (check: $\\log_3 3 + 2\\log_3 3 = 3$). $L = 2$ gives $x^2 - 5x + 7 = (x-1)^2$, so $x = 2$, which is not allowed. The only solution is $x = 4$. Forgetting that a base cannot be $1$ gives $6$ or $8$.<\/p>", "tags": ["explog"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "EA2-7", "paper": "EA2", "n": 7, "q": "<p>For which real values of $k$ does the equation \\[\\dfrac{x^2 - 4}{x^2 - 1} = k\\] have exactly two distinct real solutions?<\/p>", "opts": ["$k \\le 1$ or $k > 4$", "$1 &lt; k &lt; 4$", "$k &lt; 1$ or $k > 4$", "$k &lt; 1$ or $k \\ge 4$", "$k > 4$", "$k &lt; 1$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Rearranging, $x^2(1 - k) = 4 - k$. For $k = 1$ there is no solution. Otherwise $x^2 = \\dfrac{4-k}{1-k}$, which is never equal to $1$, so the excluded values $x = \\pm 1$ never occur. A positive value of $x^2$ gives two solutions, zero gives one and a negative value gives none. The fraction is positive for $k &lt; 1$ or $k > 4$, zero for $k = 4$ (only $x = 0$) and negative for $1 &lt; k &lt; 4$. So there are exactly two solutions when $k &lt; 1$ or $k > 4$. The value $k = 4$ is the local maximum of the curve, touched at the single point $x = 0$.<\/p>", "tags": ["graphs", "alg"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "EA2-8", "paper": "EA2", "n": 8, "q": "<p>The function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = x^2 - 6x + 10$ for $x \\ge 3$, and $f^{-1}$ is its inverse function.<\/p><p>What is the sum of all real solutions of the equation $f(x) = f^{-1}(x)$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$5$", "$7$", "$\\dfrac{15 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}$", "$2$", "$12$", "$\\dfrac{5 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>$f$ is increasing on its domain. If $f(x) = f^{-1}(x) = y$ then $f(y) = x$; if $x &lt; y$ then $f(x) &lt; f(y)$, that is $y &lt; x$, a contradiction, and $x > y$ fails in the same way. So $y = x$ and $f(x) = x$, giving $x^2 - 7x + 10 = 0$, so $x = 2$ or $x = 5$, and only $x = 5$ lies in the domain. Solving $f(f(x)) = x$ instead also produces the roots of $x^2 - 5x + 5 = 0$; the root $\\dfrac{5+\\sqrt{5}}{2} \\approx 3.6$ is in the domain, but there $f(x) = \\dfrac{5-\\sqrt{5}}{2}$, which is not $f^{-1}(x)$ because $f^{-1}$ only takes values $\\ge 3$.<\/p>", "tags": ["alg"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "EA2-9", "paper": "EA2", "n": 9, "q": "<p>How many values of $x$ in the interval $0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi$ satisfy $\\sin 5x = \\sin x$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$8$", "$11$", "$10$", "$7$", "$5$", "$9$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>$\\sin 5x = \\sin x$ means $5x = x + 2k\\pi$ or $5x = \\pi - x + 2k\\pi$. The first gives $x = \\dfrac{k\\pi}{2}$: five values $0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\pi, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}, 2\\pi$. The second gives $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} + \\dfrac{k\\pi}{3}$: six values $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}$. The values $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$ appear in both lists, so there are $5 + 6 - 2 = 9$ solutions. Missing the overlap gives $11$.<\/p>", "tags": ["trig"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "EA2-10", "paper": "EA2", "n": 10, "q": "<p>The curve $y = x^2 - 2x$ and the line $y = mx$ enclose a finite region of area $36$. What is the sum of all possible values of the constant $m$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$-4$", "$-8$", "$0$", "$4$", "$12$", "$-2$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>They meet where $x^2 - (m+2)x = 0$, at $x = 0$ and $x = m + 2$. The area between $y = mx$ and the parabola is $\\left|\\displaystyle\\int_0^{m+2} \\left((m+2)x - x^2\\right) dx\\right| = \\dfrac{|m+2|^3}{6}$. Setting this equal to $36$ gives $|m + 2| = 6$, so $m = 4$ or $m = -8$, with sum $-4$. The negative value is easy to miss, as is the shift by $2$ (which gives $m = \\pm 6$ and sum $0$).<\/p>", "tags": ["int"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "EA2-11", "paper": "EA2", "n": 11, "q": "<p>For a real constant $c$, the shortest distance from the point $(0, c)$ to the curve $y = x^2$ is exactly $1$. What is the sum of all possible values of $c$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{1}{4}$", "$-1$", "$1$", "$0$", "$\\dfrac{5}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{9}{4}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>The squared distance to $(x, x^2)$ is $x^2 + (x^2 - c)^2 = u^2 + (1 - 2c)u + c^2$, where $u = x^2 \\ge 0$. If $c \\le \\dfrac{1}{2}$ this increases for $u \\ge 0$, so the nearest point is the origin and the distance is $|c|$; with $c \\le \\dfrac{1}{2}$ this gives only $c = -1$. If $c > \\dfrac{1}{2}$ the minimum is at $u = c - \\dfrac{1}{2}$ and equals $c^2 - \\dfrac{(1 - 2c)^2}{4} = c - \\dfrac{1}{4}$, so $c = \\dfrac{5}{4}$. The sum is $\\dfrac{1}{4}$. The tempting $c = 1$ fails: from $(0,1)$ the nearest points are $\\left(\\pm\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}, \\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)$, at distance $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "coord"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "EA2-12", "paper": "EA2", "n": 12, "q": "<p>The points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $(0,0)$ and $(3,0)$. A point $P$, not on the $x$-axis, moves so that $PA = 2PB$.<\/p><p>What is the greatest possible area of triangle $PAB$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$6$", "$3$", "$\\dfrac{9}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{3}{2}$", "$4$", "$2$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>With $P(x,y)$, $x^2 + y^2 = 4\\left((x-3)^2 + y^2\\right)$ simplifies to $x^2 + y^2 - 8x + 12 = 0$, that is $(x-4)^2 + y^2 = 4$, a circle of radius $2$. The base $AB = 3$ is fixed, and the height $|y|$ is at most $2$, reached at $(4, \\pm 2)$. The greatest area is $\\dfrac{1}{2}\\times 3\\times 2 = 3$.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "EA2-13", "paper": "EA2", "n": 13, "q": "<p>Let $a = \\log_2 3$, $b = \\log_3 5$ and $c = \\log_5 8$. Which of the following is true?<\/p>", "opts": ["$c > b > a$", "$b > a > c$", "$a > b > c$", "$c > a > b$", "$a > c > b$", "$b > c > a$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Compare each with simple fractions. Since $3^2 > 2^3$, $a > \\dfrac{3}{2}$; since $5^2 &lt; 3^3$, $b &lt; \\dfrac{3}{2}$. So $a > b$. Next, $5^3 = 125 > 81 = 3^4$, so $b > \\dfrac{4}{3}$, while $8^3 = 512 &lt; 625 = 5^4$, so $c &lt; \\dfrac{4}{3}$. Hence $a > b > c$.<\/p>", "tags": ["explog"], "rating": 7.1}, {"id": "EA2-14", "paper": "EA2", "n": 14, "q": "<p>What is the smallest positive integer $n$ for which \\[\\sum_{k=1}^{n} \\log_2\\left(\\dfrac{k+2}{k}\\right) > 10\\,?\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$44$", "$63$", "$1023$", "$31$", "$45$", "$43$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>The sum is $\\log_2$ of the product $\\dfrac{3}{1}\\cdot\\dfrac{4}{2}\\cdot\\dfrac{5}{3}\\cdots\\dfrac{n+2}{n}$. The numerators are $3, 4, \\dots, n+2$ and the denominators are $1, 2, \\dots, n$, so everything cancels except $\\dfrac{(n+1)(n+2)}{1\\times 2}$. We need $(n+1)(n+2) > 2^{11} = 2048$. Since $44\\times 45 = 1980$ and $45\\times 46 = 2070$, the answer is $n = 44$. Cancelling as if adjacent terms telescoped leaves $n + 2$ and gives $1023$; forgetting the $2$ gives $31$.<\/p>", "tags": ["seq", "explog"], "rating": 7.1}, {"id": "EA2-15", "paper": "EA2", "n": 15, "q": "<p>The curve $y = x^4 - 4x^3 + ax^2$, where $a$ is a real constant, has exactly one stationary point. Which of the following describes all possible values of $a$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$a &lt; \\dfrac{9}{2}$", "$a \\ge \\dfrac{9}{2}$", "$a > \\dfrac{9}{2}$ or $a = 0$", "$a \\ge \\dfrac{9}{2}$ or $a = 0$", "$a > 9$", "$a > \\dfrac{9}{2}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>$\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = 4x^3 - 12x^2 + 2ax = 2x(2x^2 - 6x + a)$, so $x = 0$ is always a stationary point. There must be no other, so $2x^2 - 6x + a = 0$ must have no root other than $0$. If $36 - 8a &lt; 0$, that is $a > \\dfrac{9}{2}$, it has no roots. If $a = \\dfrac{9}{2}$ it has the repeated root $x = \\dfrac{3}{2}$, which is a stationary point of inflexion, still a stationary point, so there are two. If $a = 0$ the roots are $0$ and $3$, again two stationary points. So $a > \\dfrac{9}{2}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff"], "rating": 7.1}, {"id": "EA2-16", "paper": "EA2", "n": 16, "q": "<p>How many pairs $(n, x)$, where $n$ is an integer and $0 \\le x \\le \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$, satisfy \\[3\\sin x + 4\\cos x = n\\,?\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$14$", "$11$", "$12$", "$15$", "$13$", "$16$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Write $3\\sin x + 4\\cos x = 5\\sin(x + \\alpha)$, where $\\alpha$ is acute with $\\sin\\alpha = \\dfrac{4}{5}$. As $x$ runs from $0$ to $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$, $x + \\alpha$ runs from $\\alpha$ to $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2} + \\alpha$, so the value starts at $4$, rises to $5$, falls to $-5$ (when $x + \\alpha = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$), then rises to $-3$ at $x = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$. Counting: $n = 5$ once; $n = 4$ twice ($x = 0$ and once on the way down); each of $3, 2, \\dots, -4$ once on the way down, $8$ pairs; $n = -5$ once; then $-4$ and $-3$ again on the way up. The total is $1 + 2 + 8 + 1 + 2 = 14$. Counting each attainable integer once gives $11$.<\/p>", "tags": ["trig"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "EA2-17", "paper": "EA2", "n": 17, "q": "<p>What is the total area of the regions enclosed between the curves $y = \\sin x$ and $y = \\sin 2x$ for $0 \\le x \\le \\pi$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{9}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{5}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{2}$", "$3$", "$2$", "$\\dfrac{7}{2}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>The curves meet where $\\sin x(2\\cos x - 1) = 0$, at $x = 0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}, \\pi$. On $\\left[0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right]$, $\\sin 2x$ is on top: $\\left[-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\cos 2x + \\cos x\\right]_0^{\\pi/3} = \\dfrac{3}{4} - \\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{4}$. On $\\left[\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}, \\pi\\right]$, $\\sin x$ is on top: $\\left[-\\cos x + \\dfrac{1}{2}\\cos 2x\\right]_{\\pi/3}^{\\pi} = \\dfrac{3}{2} + \\dfrac{3}{4} = \\dfrac{9}{4}$. The total is $\\dfrac{5}{2}$. Integrating $\\sin x - \\sin 2x$ over the whole interval gives $2$, because the small region is counted as negative.<\/p>", "tags": ["int", "trig"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "EA2-18", "paper": "EA2", "n": 18, "q": "<p>Consider the equation $x^2 - kx + k + 3 = 0$, where $k$ is an integer.<\/p><p>What is the sum of all values of $k$ for which the equation has two integer roots? (The two roots are allowed to be equal.)<\/p>", "opts": ["$11$", "$10$", "$7$", "$4$", "$13$", "$8$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>If the roots are $r$ and $s$, then $r + s = k$ and $rs = k + 3$, so $rs - r - s = 3$, that is $(r-1)(s-1) = 4$. The factorisations $1\\times 4$, $2\\times 2$, $(-1)\\times(-4)$, $(-2)\\times(-2)$ give root pairs $\\{2,5\\}$, $\\{3,3\\}$, $\\{0,-3\\}$, $\\{-1,-1\\}$, and so $k = 7, 6, -3, -2$. The sum is $8$. Forgetting negative factors gives $13$; forgetting repeated roots gives $4$.<\/p>", "tags": ["alg"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "EA2-19", "paper": "EA2", "n": 19, "q": "<p>Some circles touch both of the lines $y = 0$ and $3y = 4x$, and also pass through the point $(8, 1)$. What is the sum of the radii of all such circles?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{17}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{104}{9}$", "$\\dfrac{17}{2}$", "$9$", "$17$", "$\\dfrac{65}{4}$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The point $(8,1)$ lies inside the angle between the positive $x$-axis and the ray along $(3,4)$. A circle touching both lines cannot cross either of them, so it lies inside this angle and its centre is on the angle bisector. Adding unit vectors, $(1,0) + \\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}, \\dfrac{4}{5}\\right) = \\left(\\dfrac{8}{5}, \\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)$, so the bisector has direction $(2,1)$ and a circle of radius $r$ has centre $(2r, r)$ (check: its distance from $4x - 3y = 0$ is $\\dfrac{|8r - 3r|}{5} = r$). Passing through $(8,1)$ gives $(8 - 2r)^2 + (1 - r)^2 = r^2$, that is $4r^2 - 34r + 65 = 0$. The discriminant $34^2 - 16\\times 65 = 116$ is positive and both roots are positive, so the sum of the radii is $\\dfrac{34}{4} = \\dfrac{17}{2}$. Taking the bisector to have the average gradient $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ gives $\\dfrac{104}{9}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord", "trig"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "EA2-20", "paper": "EA2", "n": 20, "q": "<p>What is the sum of all real solutions of the equation $\\sqrt{x + 6} = x^2 - 6$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{5 - \\sqrt{21}}{2}$", "$3$", "$0$", "$\\dfrac{5 + \\sqrt{21}}{2}$", "$1$", "$\\dfrac{-1 - \\sqrt{21}}{2}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Let $y = \\sqrt{x + 6} \\ge 0$. Then $y^2 = x + 6$ and $y = x^2 - 6$; subtracting, $y^2 - x^2 = x - y$, so $(y - x)(y + x + 1) = 0$. If $y = x$, then $x^2 - x - 6 = 0$, so $x = 3$ or $x = -2$, and $y = x \\ge 0$ leaves $x = 3$. If $y = -1 - x$, then $x^2 + x - 5 = 0$, so $x = \\dfrac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{21}}{2}$, and $y \\ge 0$ needs $x \\le -1$, leaving $x = \\dfrac{-1 - \\sqrt{21}}{2}$. The sum is $\\dfrac{5 - \\sqrt{21}}{2}$. Squaring gives the quartic $x^4 - 12x^2 - x + 30 = 0$; all four of its roots are real and add to $0$, but two of them are extraneous.<\/p>", "tags": ["alg"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "EA2-21", "paper": "EA2", "n": 21, "q": "<p>Let $f(x) = x^3 - 3x$. How many distinct real solutions does the equation $f(f(x)) = 2$ have?<\/p>", "opts": ["$4$", "$6$", "$7$", "$3$", "$5$", "$9$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Let $u = f(x)$. Then $u^3 - 3u - 2 = (u+1)^2(u-2) = 0$, so $u = -1$ or $u = 2$. The graph of $f$ has a local maximum at $(-1, 2)$ and a local minimum at $(1, -2)$. Since $-2 &lt; -1 &lt; 2$, the line $y = -1$ meets the graph three times. The line $y = 2$ touches the graph at the maximum $x = -1$ and crosses it once more at $x = 2$, giving only two distinct solutions. The total is $3 + 2 = 5$. Assuming every horizontal line between turning values gives three solutions leads to $6$, and counting the degree gives $9$.<\/p>", "tags": ["graphs"], "rating": 7.5}, {"id": "EA2-22", "paper": "EA2", "n": 22, "q": "<p>The line $y=mx$, where $m>0$, meets the curve $y=x^3-6x^2+9x$ at three distinct points, and the line and the curve enclose two regions of equal area. Find $m$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$2$", "$1$", "$\\dfrac94$", "$3$", "$4$", "$\\dfrac12$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>No integration is needed. Every cubic is symmetric under a half turn about its point of inflection. Here $y''=6x-12=0$ at $x=2$, where $y=8-24+18=2$, so the half turn about $(2,2)$ maps the curve to itself. It also maps any line through $(2,2)$ to itself, so such a line cuts off two congruent regions, one on each side of $(2,2)$. The line through the origin and $(2,2)$ is $y=x$, so $m=1$. Check: $x^3-6x^2+9x=x$ gives $x(x-2)(x-4)=0$, three distinct points placed symmetrically about $x=2$. It is the only such line: for $0&lt;m&lt;9$ the other intersections are $x=3\\pm\\sqrt m$, and making the signed area from $0$ to $b=3+\\sqrt m$ vanish gives $b^2-8b+18-2m=0$, which with $m=(b-3)^2$ becomes $b(b-4)=0$, so $b=4$ and $m=1$; for $m>9$ the region to the left of the origin is always much the smaller. Working out one area and halving, or using the tangent gradient at the origin ($9$), leads to the wrong options.<\/p>", "tags": ["int", "graphs"], "rating": 7.5}, {"id": "EA2-23", "paper": "EA2", "n": 23, "q": "<p>What is the sum of all values of $x$ in the interval $0 \\le x \\le \\pi$ that satisfy \\[\\sin x + \\sin 2x + \\sin 3x = \\cos x + \\cos 2x + \\cos 3x\\,?\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{11\\pi}{12}$", "$\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{17\\pi}{12}$", "$\\dfrac{19\\pi}{24}$", "$\\dfrac{61\\pi}{24}$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Expanding $\\sin(2x \\pm x)$ and $\\cos(2x \\pm x)$ gives $\\sin x + \\sin 3x = 2\\sin 2x\\cos x$ and $\\cos x + \\cos 3x = 2\\cos 2x\\cos x$. So the equation is $(1 + 2\\cos x)(\\sin 2x - \\cos 2x) = 0$. Either $\\cos x = -\\dfrac{1}{2}$, giving $x = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$, or $\\tan 2x = 1$ with $0 \\le 2x \\le 2\\pi$, giving $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{8}, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{8}$. The sum is $\\dfrac{17\\pi}{12}$. Cancelling the common factor $1 + 2\\cos x$ loses $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ and gives $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["trig"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "EA2-24", "paper": "EA2", "n": 24, "q": "<p>What is the value of \\[\\binom{10}{0} + \\dfrac{1}{2}\\binom{10}{1} + \\dfrac{1}{3}\\binom{10}{2} + \\dfrac{1}{4}\\binom{10}{3} + \\dots + \\dfrac{1}{11}\\binom{10}{10}\\,?\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{1024}{11}$", "$\\dfrac{1023}{11}$", "$\\dfrac{2047}{10}$", "$\\dfrac{2048}{11}$", "$\\dfrac{2047}{11}$", "$\\dfrac{1023}{10}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Integrate the binomial expansion $(1+x)^{10} = \\displaystyle\\sum_{k=0}^{10} \\binom{10}{k}x^k$ from $0$ to $1$. Each term gives $\\dfrac{1}{k+1}\\binom{10}{k}$, so the sum is $\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 (1+x)^{10}\\,dx = \\left[\\dfrac{(1+x)^{11}}{11}\\right]_0^1 = \\dfrac{2^{11} - 1}{11} = \\dfrac{2047}{11}$. Forgetting the lower limit gives $\\dfrac{2048}{11}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["seq", "int"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "EA2-25", "paper": "EA2", "n": 25, "q": "<p>For which positive values of $k$ does the equation $e^x = kx^2$ have exactly three real solutions?<\/p>", "opts": ["$0 &lt; k &lt; \\dfrac{e^2}{4}$", "$k > \\dfrac{e}{2}$", "$k \\ge \\dfrac{e^2}{4}$", "$k > \\dfrac{e^2}{2}$", "$k > \\dfrac{e^2}{4}$", "$k > e$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Note $x = 0$ is never a solution. For $x &lt; 0$, $g(x) = e^x - kx^2$ has $g'(x) = e^x - 2kx > 0$, with $g(0) = 1 > 0$ and $g(x) \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to -\\infty$, so there is exactly one negative solution for every $k > 0$. For $x > 0$, the equation is $h(x) = \\dfrac{e^x}{x^2} = k$, and $h'(x) = \\dfrac{e^x(x - 2)}{x^3}$. So $h$ falls from $+\\infty$ to its minimum $h(2) = \\dfrac{e^2}{4}$ and then rises to $+\\infty$, giving two positive solutions when $k > \\dfrac{e^2}{4}$ and one when $k = \\dfrac{e^2}{4}$. Exactly three solutions occur for $k > \\dfrac{e^2}{4}$. The tempting $k \\ge \\dfrac{e^2}{4}$ fails because at $k = \\dfrac{e^2}{4}$ the curves only touch at $x = 2$, giving two solutions in total.<\/p>", "tags": ["explog", "graphs"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "EA2-26", "paper": "EA2", "n": 26, "q": "<p>The circles $x^2 + y^2 = 1$ and $(x-4)^2 + y^2 = 4$ have exactly four common tangents. What is the product of the gradients of these four lines?<\/p>", "opts": ["$-\\dfrac{3}{35}$", "$-\\dfrac{9}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{9}{35}$", "$-\\dfrac{1}{15}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{105}$", "$\\dfrac{3}{35}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>No vertical line touches both circles, so each common tangent is $y = mx + c$. Tangency to the first circle gives $c^2 = 1 + m^2$, and to the second gives $(4m + c)^2 = 4(1 + m^2) = 4c^2$. So $4m + c = \\pm 2c$, that is $c = 4m$ or $c = -\\dfrac{4m}{3}$. Then $16m^2 = 1 + m^2$ gives $m^2 = \\dfrac{1}{15}$, and $\\dfrac{16m^2}{9} = 1 + m^2$ gives $m^2 = \\dfrac{9}{7}$. The gradients are $\\pm\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{15}}$ and $\\pm\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{7}}$, with product $\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{15}\\right)\\left(-\\dfrac{9}{7}\\right) = \\dfrac{3}{35}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord"], "rating": 8.2}, {"id": "EA2-27", "paper": "EA2", "n": 27, "q": "<p>For how many integer values of $k$ do exactly three distinct tangents to the curve $y = x^3 - x$ pass through the point $(2, k)$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$5$", "$3$", "$9$", "$8$", "$7$", "$6$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>The tangent at $x = t$ is $y = (3t^2 - 1)(x - t) + t^3 - t = (3t^2 - 1)x - 2t^3$. It passes through $(2,k)$ when $p(t) = 2t^3 - 6t^2 + 2 + k = 0$. A line cannot touch a cubic at two different points, so distinct values of $t$ give distinct tangents, and we need three distinct real roots. As $p'(t) = 6t(t - 2)$, this happens when the local maximum $p(0) = 2 + k$ is positive and the local minimum $p(2) = k - 6$ is negative, so $-2 &lt; k &lt; 6$. That is $7$ integers. At $k = -2$ and $k = 6$ (the point $(2,6)$ is on the curve) there are only two tangents.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "graphs"], "rating": 8.6}, {"id": "EB1-1", "paper": "EB1", "n": 1, "q": "<p>A surveyor fixes three beacons at the points $A(-1,4)$, $B(6,5)$ and $C(7,-2)$, with all distances measured in kilometres. A relay mast $M$ is to be placed so that it lies on the locus of points equidistant from $A$ and $B$, and also on the locus of points equidistant from $B$ and $C$. How far is the mast from the beacon $B$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$4\\sqrt{2}\\text{ km}$", "$\\sqrt{29}\\text{ km}$", "$10\\text{ km}$", "$5\\sqrt{2}\\text{ km}$", "$5\\text{ km}$", "$\\sqrt{26}\\text{ km}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>$M$ is equidistant from all three beacons, so it is the centre of the circle through $A$, $B$ and $C$. The gradient of $AB$ is $\\tfrac17$ and the gradient of $BC$ is $-7$; their product is $-1$, so $\\angle ABC=90^\\circ$. By the angle in a semicircle, $AC$ is a diameter, so $M$ is the midpoint $(3,1)$ of $AC$ and $MB=\\tfrac12AC=\\tfrac12\\sqrt{8^2+6^2}=5$ km. Solving the two perpendicular bisectors, $y=-7x+22$ and $x-7y=-4$, gives the same point, but the right angle saves the algebra. The option $10$ km is the diameter $AC$ itself.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry", "algebra"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "EB1-2", "paper": "EB1", "n": 2, "q": "<p>A foundry makes an alloy by melting together two metals: metal $P$, of density $9\\text{ g/cm}^3$, and metal $Q$, of density $6\\text{ g/cm}^3$. Assume that when the metals are melted together their volumes simply add. An ingot of the alloy has mass $1.8\\text{ kg}$ and density $7.2\\text{ g/cm}^3$. More of metal $P$ is then melted into the ingot until the density of the new, larger ingot is $7.5\\text{ g/cm}^3$. What fraction of the mass of the new ingot is metal $P$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{3}{5}$", "$\\dfrac{2}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{5}{8}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{9}{16}$", "$\\dfrac{2}{5}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>The original volume is $1800\\div7.2=250\\text{ cm}^3$. If it contains $v\\text{ cm}^3$ of $P$, then $9v+6(250-v)=1800$, so $v=100$: the ingot holds $900$ g of $P$ and $900$ g of $Q$. Adding $x\\text{ cm}^3$ of $P$ gives $\\dfrac{1800+9x}{250+x}=7.5$, so $1.5x=75$ and $x=50$, which is $450$ g. The new ingot contains $1350$ g of $P$ out of $2250$ g, a fraction $\\tfrac35$. The trap $\\tfrac12$ is the new <em>volume<\/em> fraction ($150$ out of $300\\text{ cm}^3$), and $\\tfrac25$ is the original volume fraction.<\/p>", "tags": ["units", "ratio"], "rating": 7.3}, {"id": "EB1-3", "paper": "EB1", "n": 3, "q": "<p>A garden is in the shape of a trapezium $ABCD$, in which the side $AB$ is parallel to the side $DC$. Two straight paths run along the diagonals $AC$ and $BD$, crossing at the point $X$, and they divide the garden into four triangular flower beds. The bed $AXD$ has area $6\\text{ m}^2$ and the bed $DXC$ has area $4\\text{ m}^2$. Ignoring the width of the paths, what is the total area of the garden?<\/p>", "opts": ["$36\\text{ m}^2$", "$24\\text{ m}^2$", "$22\\text{ m}^2$", "$30\\text{ m}^2$", "$25\\text{ m}^2$", "$20\\text{ m}^2$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Triangles $ABD$ and $ABC$ share the base $AB$ and have equal heights, so removing the common part $AXB$ shows that $BXC$ also has area $6$. Triangles $DXC$ and $BXC$ have the same height from $C$, so $DX:XB=4:6=2:3$. Triangles $DXC$ and $BXA$ are similar (alternate angles), with lengths in the ratio $2:3$, so $AXB$ has area $4\\times\\tfrac94=9$. The total is $4+6+6+9=25\\text{ m}^2$. Scaling the area linearly ($AXB=6$, total $22$) or assuming a fourth bed equal to $DXC$ (total $20$) are the traps.<\/p>", "tags": ["ratio", "geometry"], "rating": 7.3}, {"id": "EB1-4", "paper": "EB1", "n": 4, "q": "<p>A cyclist rides along a measured course whose length is recorded as $0.40\\text{ km}$, correct to two significant figures. Her time is measured with a stopwatch that displays whole seconds only and works by truncation: it ignores any fraction of a second, so that, for example, a time of $50.8$ seconds is displayed as $50$. At the end of her ride the stopwatch displays $50$. What is the upper bound for her average speed over the course, in kilometres per hour?<\/p>", "opts": ["$29.52$", "$29.16$", "$32.4$", "$28.8$", "$8.1$", "$29.\\dot{4}\\dot{5}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Two significant figures in $0.40$ km means the nearest $0.01$ km, so the length $d$ satisfies $395\\le d&lt;405$ metres. Truncation means the time $t$ satisfies $50\\le t&lt;51$ seconds. The upper bound of the speed is $405\\div50=8.1\\text{ m/s}$, and $8.1\\times3.6=29.16\\text{ km/h}$. Treating the time as rounded, with lower bound $49.5$, gives $29.\\dot4\\dot5$; reading $0.40$ as one significant figure (so $d&lt;450$) gives $32.4$; and $8.1$ is the answer in metres per second.<\/p>", "tags": ["number", "units"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "EB1-5", "paper": "EB1", "n": 5, "q": "<p>A stone plinth has the shape of a frustum of a right pyramid with a square base: its lower face is a $2\\text{ m}$ by $2\\text{ m}$ square, its upper face is a $1\\text{ m}$ by $1\\text{ m}$ square parallel to it, and its perpendicular height is $0.9\\text{ m}$. The stone has density $2400\\text{ kg/m}^3$. The plinth stands on its lower face on level ground, and a bronze statue of mass $1200\\text{ kg}$ is fixed to its upper face. Taking the gravitational field strength to be $10\\text{ N/kg}$, what is the pressure exerted on the ground by the plinth and statue together?<\/p>", "opts": ["$12.6\\text{ kPa}$", "$15.6\\text{ kPa}$", "$15.15\\text{ kPa}$", "$62.4\\text{ kPa}$", "$16.5\\text{ kPa}$", "$24.6\\text{ kPa}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Extend the sloping faces to a complete pyramid. The side length halves, from $2$ m to $1$ m, over a height of $0.9$ m, so the apex is $1.8$ m above the ground and the missing top pyramid has height $0.9$ m. The volume is $\\tfrac13(4)(1.8)-\\tfrac13(1)(0.9)=2.4-0.3=2.1\\text{ m}^3$, a mass of $5040$ kg. The total mass is $6240$ kg, a weight of $62\\,400$ N, spread over $4\\text{ m}^2$: the pressure is $15\\,600$ Pa $=15.6$ kPa. Treating the frustum as a prism whose cross-section is the mean of the two faces ($16.5$) or the square of side $1.5$ m ($15.15$), or forgetting the statue ($12.6$), are the traps.<\/p>", "tags": ["units", "geometry"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "EB1-6", "paper": "EB1", "n": 6, "q": "<p>The lengths, $L$ cm, of $60$ leaves are summarised in a grouped frequency table: $8$ leaves have $0\\le L&lt;10$, $x$ leaves have $10\\le L&lt;20$, $20$ leaves have $20\\le L&lt;30$, $y$ leaves have $30\\le L&lt;40$, and $6$ leaves have $40\\le L&lt;60$. Using the midpoints of the classes, an estimate of the mean length is $24.5$ cm. The data are then drawn as a histogram, on which the bar for the class $40\\le L&lt;60$ has a height of $1.5$ cm. What is the height of the bar for the class $10\\le L&lt;20$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$3.5\\text{ cm}$", "$7\\text{ cm}$", "$10\\text{ cm}$", "$3\\text{ cm}$", "$14\\text{ cm}$", "$6\\text{ cm}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>First $x+y=26$, and using midpoints $8(5)+15x+20(25)+35y+6(50)=60\\times24.5=1470$, so $15x+35y=630$. Hence $x=14$ and $y=12$. On a histogram, height is proportional to frequency density. The last class has density $6\\div20=0.3$, drawn as $1.5$ cm, so each unit of density is $5$ cm. The class $10\\le L&lt;20$ has density $14\\div10=1.4$, so its bar is $7$ cm tall. Scaling frequencies instead of densities gives $3.5$ cm, and swapping $x$ and $y$ gives $6$ cm.<\/p>", "tags": ["stats"], "rating": 7.5}, {"id": "EB1-7", "paper": "EB1", "n": 7, "q": "<p>An isosceles trapezium $ABCD$ is inscribed in a circle of radius $13\\text{ cm}$ and centre $O$. Its parallel sides are $AB=24\\text{ cm}$ and $CD=10\\text{ cm}$, and the centre $O$ lies outside the trapezium. What is the area of the trapezium?<\/p>", "opts": ["$119\\text{ cm}^2$", "$170\\text{ cm}^2$", "$289\\text{ cm}^2$", "$84\\text{ cm}^2$", "$102\\text{ cm}^2$", "$238\\text{ cm}^2$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>The perpendicular from the centre to a chord bisects the chord, and for both parallel chords this perpendicular lies along the axis of symmetry. So $O$ is $\\sqrt{13^2-12^2}=5$ cm from $AB$ and $\\sqrt{13^2-5^2}=12$ cm from $CD$. If $O$ were inside the trapezium the height would be $5+12=17$ cm, giving the tempting $289$. Since $O$ is outside, both chords lie on the same side of $O$ and the height is $12-5=7$ cm, so the area is $\\tfrac12(24+10)(7)=119\\text{ cm}^2$.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 7.5}, {"id": "EB1-8", "paper": "EB1", "n": 8, "q": "<p>Which of the following correctly lists the five numbers \\[2^{150},\\qquad 3^{100},\\qquad 5^{60},\\qquad 7^{50},\\qquad 11^{40}\\] in increasing order?<\/p>", "opts": ["$2^{150}&lt;3^{100}&lt;5^{60}&lt;7^{50}&lt;11^{40}$", "$11^{40}&lt;5^{60}&lt;7^{50}&lt;3^{100}&lt;2^{150}$", "$5^{60}&lt;7^{50}&lt;11^{40}&lt;3^{100}&lt;2^{150}$", "$11^{40}&lt;7^{50}&lt;5^{60}&lt;2^{150}&lt;3^{100}$", "$11^{40}&lt;5^{60}&lt;7^{50}&lt;2^{150}&lt;3^{100}$", "$5^{60}&lt;11^{40}&lt;7^{50}&lt;2^{150}&lt;3^{100}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Every exponent is a multiple of $10$, so compare the tenth roots: $2^{15}=32768$, $3^{10}=59049$, $5^6=15625$, $7^5=16807$ and $11^4=14641$. Hence $11^{40}&lt;5^{60}&lt;7^{50}&lt;2^{150}&lt;3^{100}$. Ordering by base, or by exponent, fails, and the three smallest values are close enough that they have to be computed rather than estimated.<\/p>", "tags": ["number"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "EB1-9", "paper": "EB1", "n": 9, "q": "<p>A freight train travels from one depot to another. For the first half of the total journey <em>time<\/em> it travels at a constant $100\\text{ km/h}$. Of the distance that then remains, it covers the first half at a constant $60\\text{ km/h}$ and the second half at a constant $120\\text{ km/h}$. Ignoring the time taken to change speed, what is the average speed of the train for the whole journey?<\/p>", "opts": ["$85\\tfrac{5}{7}\\text{ km/h}$", "$95\\text{ km/h}$", "$90\\text{ km/h}$", "$80\\text{ km/h}$", "$93\\tfrac{1}{3}\\text{ km/h}$", "$100\\text{ km/h}$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Over two equal distances $s$ at $60$ and $120$ km/h the time is $\\tfrac{s}{60}+\\tfrac{s}{120}=\\tfrac{s}{40}$, so the average speed for the second phase is $2s\\div\\tfrac{s}{40}=80$ km/h (the harmonic mean), not $90$. The two phases last equal times, so the overall average is the ordinary mean of $100$ and $80$, namely $90$ km/h. Using $90$ for the second phase gives $95$; averaging the three speeds gives $93\\tfrac13$; treating all three stretches as equal distances gives $85\\tfrac57$.<\/p>", "tags": ["units"], "rating": 7.7}, {"id": "EB1-10", "paper": "EB1", "n": 10, "q": "<p>A screening test for a medical condition has a sensitivity of $90\\%$ (the probability that a person who has the condition tests positive) and a specificity of $95\\%$ (the probability that a person who does not have the condition tests negative). In a certain population, $2\\%$ of people have the condition. Everyone who tests positive is immediately tested a second time with the same test; for any given person, the results of the two tests are independent of each other, once it is known whether or not that person has the condition. A person chosen at random from the population tests positive on both occasions. What is the probability that this person has the condition?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{18}{67}$", "$\\dfrac{81}{100}$", "$\\dfrac{9}{10}$", "$\\dfrac{81}{326}$", "$\\dfrac{162}{187}$", "$\\dfrac{324}{373}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Per $10\\,000$ people: $200$ have the condition and $200\\times0.9\\times0.9=162$ of them test positive twice; $9800$ do not, and $9800\\times0.05\\times0.05=24.5$ of them test positive twice. The required probability is $\\dfrac{162}{162+24.5}=\\dfrac{324}{373}$. Stopping after one test gives $\\tfrac{18}{67}$; forgetting the factor $0.98$ gives $\\tfrac{162}{187}$; and $\\tfrac{81}{100}$ is the probability of two positives given the condition, the reverse conditional.<\/p>", "tags": ["prob"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "EB1-11", "paper": "EB1", "n": 11, "q": "<p>A ship sails due east in a straight line from a buoy $A$ to a buoy $B$, which is $12\\text{ km}$ from $A$. From $A$, a lighthouse $L$ is on a bearing of $030^\\circ$. From $B$, the same lighthouse is on a bearing of $315^\\circ$. What is the shortest distance between the lighthouse and the ship's path from $A$ to $B$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$6\\sqrt{3}\\text{ km}$", "$(18\\sqrt{2}-6\\sqrt{6})\\text{ km}$", "$(12\\sqrt{3}-12)\\text{ km}$", "$(6\\sqrt{3}-6)\\text{ km}$", "$4\\sqrt{3}\\text{ km}$", "$(18-6\\sqrt{3})\\text{ km}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>The bearing $030^\\circ$ makes $\\angle LAB=90^\\circ-30^\\circ=60^\\circ$, and from $B$ the direction back to $A$ is $270^\\circ$, so $\\angle LBA=315^\\circ-270^\\circ=45^\\circ$. Both angles are acute, so the foot $H$ of the perpendicular from $L$ lies between $A$ and $B$. With $LH=h$, $AH=\\dfrac{h}{\\tan60^\\circ}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt3}$ and $HB=h$, so $h\\left(1+\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\right)=12$, giving $h=\\dfrac{12\\sqrt3}{\\sqrt3+1}=6\\sqrt3(\\sqrt3-1)=18-6\\sqrt3$ km. Using the bearing $30^\\circ$ itself as the angle at $A$ gives $6\\sqrt3-6$; $12\\sqrt3-12$ is the length $AL$.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "EB1-12", "paper": "EB1", "n": 12, "q": "<p>For each real number $k$, the parabola $C_k$ has equation \\[y=x^2-2kx+4k.\\] Consider the following statements.<\/p><ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>There is a point that lies on every one of the parabolas $C_k$.<\/li><li>As $k$ varies, the turning point of $C_k$ always lies on the curve $y=4x-x^2$.<\/li><li>No parabola $C_k$ has its turning point above the line $y=4$.<\/li><li>Every parabola $C_k$ meets the $x$-axis.<\/li><\/ol><p>Which of these statements are true?<\/p>", "opts": ["II and III only", "I and II only", "I, II and III only", "I and III only", "II only", "II, III and IV only", "I, II, III and IV", "I and IV only"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Write $y=x^2+k(4-2x)$: at $x=2$ we get $y=4$ whatever the value of $k$, so every $C_k$ passes through $(2,4)$ and I is true. Completing the square, $y=(x-k)^2+4k-k^2$, so the turning point is $(k,\\,4k-k^2)$, which lies on $y=4x-x^2$: II is true. Its $y$-coordinate is $4-(k-2)^2\\le4$, so III is true. But for $0&lt;k&lt;4$ the minimum value $4k-k^2$ is positive; for example $C_2$ is $y=(x-2)^2+4$, which never meets the $x$-axis. So IV is false.<\/p>", "tags": ["algebra"], "rating": 7.9}, {"id": "EB1-13", "paper": "EB1", "n": 13, "q": "<p>In a fairground game, three fair six-sided dice, each numbered $1$ to $6$, are rolled together and the three scores are multiplied. The player wins a prize if this product is a multiple of $10$. What is the probability of winning a prize?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{11}{36}$", "$\\dfrac{91}{216}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{637}{1728}$", "$\\dfrac{8}{27}$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The product is a multiple of $10$ exactly when at least one die shows $5$ and at least one die shows an even number. For the complement: no $5$ has probability $\\tfrac{125}{216}$, no even number has probability $\\tfrac{27}{216}$, and both together (every die in $\\{1,3\\}$) has probability $\\tfrac{8}{216}$. So the answer is $1-\\tfrac{125}{216}-\\tfrac{27}{216}+\\tfrac{8}{216}=\\tfrac{72}{216}=\\tfrac13$. Forgetting to add back the overlap gives $\\tfrac{8}{27}$. Multiplying $P(\\text{some }5)=\\tfrac{91}{216}$ by $P(\\text{some even})=\\tfrac78$ gives $\\tfrac{637}{1728}$, but these events are not independent, since a die showing $5$ cannot also be even.<\/p>", "tags": ["prob"], "rating": 7.9}, {"id": "EB1-14", "paper": "EB1", "n": 14, "q": "<p>A lampshade is the curved surface of a right circular frustum, open at both ends. The radii of its two circular ends are $6\\text{ cm}$ and $10\\text{ cm}$, and the perpendicular distance between the two ends is $3\\text{ cm}$. It is made, with no overlap and no waste, by bending a flat annular sector of card: the region between two arcs of concentric circles, cut off by two radii of the larger circle. What are the angle of the annular sector and the radius of its inner arc?<\/p>", "opts": ["$300^\\circ$ and $7.5\\text{ cm}$", "$288^\\circ$ and $7.5\\text{ cm}$", "$216^\\circ$ and $4.5\\text{ cm}$", "$240^\\circ$ and $9\\text{ cm}$", "$216^\\circ$ and $7.5\\text{ cm}$", "$288^\\circ$ and $6\\text{ cm}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>The slant height of the frustum is $\\sqrt{3^2+4^2}=5$ cm. Complete the cone: its slant height $\\ell$ satisfies $\\dfrac{\\ell}{10}=\\dfrac{\\ell-5}{6}$, so $\\ell=12.5$, and the removed cone has slant height $7.5$. These slant heights are the outer and inner radii of the annular sector. The outer arc becomes the larger rim, of length $20\\pi$, so the angle is $\\dfrac{20\\pi}{2\\pi\\times12.5}\\times360^\\circ=288^\\circ$ (the inner arc checks: $\\tfrac{288}{360}\\times2\\pi\\times7.5=12\\pi$). Taking the inner radius to be the rim radius $6$ cm, or using $\\tfrac{6}{10}$ of $360^\\circ$, are the traps.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry", "ratio"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "EB1-15", "paper": "EB1", "n": 15, "q": "<p>A water trough is a prism of length $3\\text{ m}$. Its cross-section is an isosceles trapezium with a horizontal base of width $40\\text{ cm}$ at the bottom, an open top of width $100\\text{ cm}$, and a depth of $60\\text{ cm}$. The trough stands on level ground and is initially empty. Water flows in at a constant rate of $15$ litres per minute. What is the depth of the water after $20$ minutes?<\/p>", "opts": ["$10\\text{ cm}$", "$20\\text{ cm}$", "$14\\tfrac{2}{7}\\text{ cm}$", "$24\\text{ cm}$", "$16\\text{ cm}$", "$25\\text{ cm}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>In $20$ minutes $300$ litres, that is $300\\,000\\text{ cm}^3$, flows in; dividing by the length $300$ cm, the water's cross-section has area $1000\\text{ cm}^2$. The width increases by $60$ cm over a depth of $60$ cm, so at height $h$ above the base the width is $40+h$. The water's cross-section is a trapezium of area $\\tfrac12(40+40+h)h=1000$, so $h^2+80h-2000=0$, which factorises as $(h+100)(h-20)=0$, and $h=20$ cm. Treating the water as a rectangle of width $40$, $70$ or $100$ cm gives $25$, $14\\tfrac27$ or $10$ cm.<\/p>", "tags": ["units", "algebra"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "EB1-16", "paper": "EB1", "n": 16, "q": "<p>Fifteen different positive integers are arranged in increasing order. For this question the lower quartile is the $4$th value, the median is the $8$th value and the upper quartile is the $12$th value. The lower quartile is $10$, the median is $20$, the upper quartile is $30$, and the range is $40$. Let $G$ be the greatest possible value of the mean of the fifteen integers, and let $S$ be the smallest possible value. What is $G-S$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{28}{5}$", "$\\dfrac{64}{15}$", "$6$", "$\\dfrac{88}{15}$", "$\\dfrac{86}{15}$", "$\\dfrac{92}{15}$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Let the smallest value be $a$, so the largest is $a+40$, and the three values below $10$ force $a\\le7$. To maximise, take $a=7$: the values are $7,8,9,10,17,18,19,20,27,28,29,30,45,46,47$, with total $360$. Note that raising $a$ also raises the largest value, so both ends push the same way. To minimise, take $a=1$: the values are $1,2,3,10,11,12,13,20,21,22,23,30,31,32,41$, with total $272$. So $G-S=\\dfrac{360-272}{15}=\\dfrac{88}{15}$. Fixing the extremes independently, for example keeping the largest value at $41$ when maximising, leads to the other options.<\/p>", "tags": ["stats"], "rating": 8.1}, {"id": "EB1-17", "paper": "EB1", "n": 17, "q": "<p>A bag contains at least $30$ and at most $40$ counters. Each counter is red or blue, and there are more red counters than blue counters. Two counters are taken out at random, without replacement. The probability that they are the same colour is exactly $\\tfrac12$. How many red counters are in the bag?<\/p>", "opts": ["$30$", "$18$", "$24$", "$21$", "$20$", "$15$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>With $r$ red and $b$ blue counters and $n=r+b$, the condition is $\\dfrac{r(r-1)+b(b-1)}{n(n-1)}=\\dfrac12$, so $2r^2+2b^2-2n=n^2-n=(r+b)^2-(r+b)$. This simplifies to $(r-b)^2=r+b$. So $n$ must be a perfect square, and the only square from $30$ to $40$ is $36$, giving $r-b=6$, $r+b=36$, so $r=21$. The option $15$ comes from $n=25$, which is outside the allowed range.<\/p>", "tags": ["prob", "algebra"], "rating": 8.1}, {"id": "EB1-18", "paper": "EB1", "n": 18, "q": "<p>A packaging company makes closed cuboid boxes whose three edge lengths, measured in centimetres, are all whole numbers, and whose volume is exactly $360\\text{ cm}^3$. Two boxes count as the same design if one is congruent to the other, that is, if they have the same three edge lengths in some order. How many different designs are possible?<\/p>", "opts": ["$30$", "$34$", "$36$", "$27$", "$180$", "$32$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Count triples $a\\le b\\le c$ with $abc=360=2^3\\times3^2\\times5$, listing by the smallest edge $a$. For $a=1$: $bc=360$, which has $24$ divisors, giving $12$ pairs. For $a=2$: $bc=180$ with $b\\ge2$, giving $9-1=8$. For $a=3$: $bc=120$ with $b\\ge3$, giving $8-2=6$. For $a=4$: $bc=90$ with $4\\le b\\le9$, so $b=5,6,9$, giving $3$. For $a=5$: $bc=72$ with $5\\le b\\le8$, so $b=6,8$, giving $2$. For $a=6$: $bc=60$ with $b=6$ only, giving $1$. For $a\\ge7$ there is nothing, since $a^3\\le360$ and $7$ does not divide $360$. The total is $12+8+6+3+2+1=32$. The number of <em>ordered<\/em> triples is $180$.<\/p>", "tags": ["number"], "rating": 8.2}, {"id": "EB1-19", "paper": "EB1", "n": 19, "q": "<p>$ABCDEF$ is a regular hexagon, with its vertices labelled in order. Let $\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AF}=\\mathbf{b}$. The point $M$ is the midpoint of the side $CD$, and the straight line through $A$ and $M$ meets the diagonal $BF$ at the point $P$. Which of the following is equal to $\\overrightarrow{AP}$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{5}{7}\\mathbf{a}+\\dfrac{2}{7}\\mathbf{b}$", "$\\dfrac{4}{5}\\mathbf{a}+\\dfrac{3}{5}\\mathbf{b}$", "$\\dfrac{2}{3}\\mathbf{a}+\\dfrac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}$", "$\\dfrac{4}{7}\\mathbf{a}+\\dfrac{3}{7}\\mathbf{b}$", "$\\dfrac{3}{5}\\mathbf{a}+\\dfrac{2}{5}\\mathbf{b}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}+\\dfrac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>The hexagon is made of six equilateral triangles about its centre $O$, so $\\overrightarrow{AO}=\\mathbf a+\\mathbf b$ and $\\overrightarrow{BC}=\\overrightarrow{AO}$. Hence $\\overrightarrow{AC}=2\\mathbf a+\\mathbf b$; also $\\overrightarrow{CD}=\\overrightarrow{AF}=\\mathbf b$, so $\\overrightarrow{AM}=2\\mathbf a+\\tfrac32\\mathbf b$. A point on $AM$ is $t\\left(2\\mathbf a+\\tfrac32\\mathbf b\\right)$ and a point on $BF$ is $\\mathbf a+u(\\mathbf b-\\mathbf a)=(1-u)\\mathbf a+u\\mathbf b$. Since $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b$ are not parallel, $2t=1-u$ and $\\tfrac32t=u$, so $t=\\tfrac27$ and $\\overrightarrow{AP}=\\tfrac47\\mathbf a+\\tfrac37\\mathbf b$. The option $\\tfrac35\\mathbf a+\\tfrac25\\mathbf b$ also lies on $BF$ but not on $AM$; $\\tfrac12\\mathbf a+\\tfrac12\\mathbf b$ is the midpoint of $BF$.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 8.2}, {"id": "EB1-20", "paper": "EB1", "n": 20, "q": "<p>A triangular field $ABC$ has $BC=12\\text{ m}$. Two straight fences, each parallel to $BC$ and each running from the side $AB$ to the side $AC$, divide the field into three regions whose areas, taken in order starting from the vertex $A$, are in the ratio $1:2:3$. What is the total length of the two fences?<\/p>", "opts": ["$(4\\sqrt{3}+4\\sqrt{6})\\text{ m}$", "$8\\text{ m}$", "$(2\\sqrt{3}+6\\sqrt{2})\\text{ m}$", "$(6\\sqrt{2}+2\\sqrt{30})\\text{ m}$", "$(2\\sqrt{6}+6\\sqrt{2})\\text{ m}$", "$(4\\sqrt{6}+6\\sqrt{2})\\text{ m}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Each fence cuts off at $A$ a triangle similar to $ABC$. The nearer fence cuts off $\\tfrac16$ of the area and the further one cuts off $\\tfrac16+\\tfrac26=\\tfrac12$. Lengths scale as the square root of areas, so the fences have lengths $12\\sqrt{\\tfrac16}=2\\sqrt6$ and $12\\sqrt{\\tfrac12}=6\\sqrt2$, a total of $2\\sqrt6+6\\sqrt2$ m. Taking lengths proportional to areas gives $2+6=8$; reading the ratio from $BC$ instead of from $A$ gives $6\\sqrt2+2\\sqrt{30}$; equal areas would give $4\\sqrt3+4\\sqrt6$.<\/p>", "tags": ["ratio", "geometry"], "rating": 8.3}, {"id": "EB1-21", "paper": "EB1", "n": 21, "q": "<p>A rectangular sheet of card has sides in the ratio $1:\\sqrt{2}$. A guillotine is used repeatedly as follows: each time, the largest possible square is cut off from the rectangle that remains (one side of the square lying along a shorter side of the rectangle), and the square is thrown away. After three squares have been removed in this way, what fraction of the area of the original sheet remains?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{10-7\\sqrt{2}}{2}$", "$5\\sqrt{2}-7$", "$17-12\\sqrt{2}$", "$\\dfrac{7-4\\sqrt{2}}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{3\\sqrt{2}-4}{2}$", "$3-2\\sqrt{2}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Take the sides as $1$ and $\\sqrt2$, area $\\sqrt2$. The first cut leaves a $1$ by $\\sqrt2-1$ strip. Since $\\sqrt2-1&lt;1$, the second square has side $\\sqrt2-1$ and leaves $(\\sqrt2-1)$ by $(2-\\sqrt2)$. Since $\\sqrt2-1&lt;2-\\sqrt2$, the third square has side $\\sqrt2-1$ again and leaves $(\\sqrt2-1)$ by $(3-2\\sqrt2)=(\\sqrt2-1)^2$. The remaining area is $(\\sqrt2-1)^3=5\\sqrt2-7$, and as a fraction of $\\sqrt2$ this is $\\dfrac{5\\sqrt2-7}{\\sqrt2}=5-\\dfrac{7}{\\sqrt2}=\\dfrac{10-7\\sqrt2}{2}$. Forgetting to divide by the original area gives $5\\sqrt2-7$; $3-2\\sqrt2$ is the fraction after only two cuts.<\/p>", "tags": ["number", "ratio"], "rating": 8.4}, {"id": "EB1-22", "paper": "EB1", "n": 22, "q": "<p>In a Fibonacci-type sequence, each term from the third onwards is the sum of the two terms immediately before it. The first two terms of such a sequence are positive integers (not necessarily different), and its $10$th term is $2026$. How many such sequences are there?<\/p>", "opts": ["$5$", "$2$", "$1$", "$4$", "$6$", "$3$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>With first two terms $a$ and $b$, the terms are $a,\\ b,\\ a+b,\\ a+2b,\\ 2a+3b,\\ 3a+5b,\\ 5a+8b,\\ 8a+13b,\\ 13a+21b,\\ 21a+34b$. So we need $21a+34b=2026$ with $a,b\\ge1$, that is, $2026-34b$ must be a positive multiple of $21$. As $2026=21\\times96+10$ and $34b=21b+13b$, this needs $13b-10$ to be a multiple of $21$. The smallest such $b$ is $4$ (since $52-10=42$), and then $b$ can go up in steps of $21$. The values $b=4,25,46$ give $a=90,56,22$, while $b=67$ makes $a$ negative. So there are $3$ sequences.<\/p>", "tags": ["algebra", "number"], "rating": 8.5}, {"id": "EB1-23", "paper": "EB1", "n": 23, "q": "<p>Seven positive integers have a mean of $10$ and a median of $9$, and the number $12$ is their only mode. Let $R$ be the greatest possible range of the seven integers, and let $r$ be the least possible range. What is $R-r$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$24$", "$27$", "$29$", "$20$", "$26$", "$30$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>In order the integers are $a\\le b\\le c\\le9\\le e\\le f\\le g$ with the middle one equal to $9$ and total $70$. Since $12$ is a mode it appears at least twice, and as $12>9$ it fills at least two of the places $e,f,g$. Greatest range: take $e=f=12$, with $12$ appearing exactly twice, so every other value may appear only once; then $a,b,c$ are at least $1,2,3$ and $g=70-(1+2+3+9+24)=31$, so $R=30$. (Taking $a=b=c=1$ would make $1$ a mode too.) Least range: with $e=f=g=12$, $a+b+c=25$ with $c\\le9$; no value other than $12$ may appear three times, so $(7,9,9)$ fails and the best is $(8,8,9)$, giving $r=12-8=4$. If $12$ appears only twice, every other value appears once, so $c\\le8$ and $a+b+c\\le6+7+8=21$; this forces $e=f=12$ and $g\\ge16$, and the range is at least $10$. So $R-r=26$.<\/p>", "tags": ["stats"], "rating": 8.6}, {"id": "EB1-24", "paper": "EB1", "n": 24, "q": "<p>A tiler wants to fit three regular polygons together around a point $P$, so that each polygon has a vertex at $P$, no two of the polygons overlap, and together they fill the whole angle around $P$ with no gaps. No two of the three polygons may have the same number of sides. How many different sets of three polygons, described by their numbers of sides, are possible?<\/p>", "opts": ["$5$", "$7$", "$10$", "$8$", "$4$", "$6$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>A regular $n$-gon has interior angle $180^\\circ-\\dfrac{360^\\circ}{n}$, so the condition becomes $\\dfrac1a+\\dfrac1b+\\dfrac1c=\\dfrac12$ with $3\\le a&lt;b&lt;c$. Since $\\dfrac3a>\\dfrac12$, $a\\le5$. For $a=3$: $(b-6)(c-6)=36$, giving $(7,42),(8,24),(9,18),(10,15)$. For $a=4$: $(b-4)(c-4)=16$, giving $(5,20),(6,12)$ (the factorisation $4\\times4$ would make $b=c$). For $a=5$: $\\dfrac1b+\\dfrac1c=\\dfrac3{10}$ needs $b&lt;\\dfrac{20}{3}$, so $b=6$, but then $c=\\tfrac{15}{2}$. That makes $6$ sets. Allowing repeated polygons would add $\\{3,12,12\\},\\{4,8,8\\},\\{5,5,10\\},\\{6,6,6\\}$, giving the trap $10$.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry", "number"], "rating": 8.7}, {"id": "EB1-25", "paper": "EB1", "n": 25, "q": "<p>In a tennis match between Ana and Bea, the first player to win three sets wins the match, and no further sets are played once the match has been decided. Ana wins each set with probability $\\tfrac23$, independently of the results of all other sets. Given that Ana wins the match, what is the probability that the match lasted exactly four sets?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{8}{27}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{3}{8}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{10}{27}$", "$\\dfrac{4}{5}$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>For Ana to win in exactly $n$ sets she must win the $n$th set and exactly two of the first $n-1$. In three sets: $\\left(\\tfrac23\\right)^3=\\tfrac{8}{27}$. In four: $3\\left(\\tfrac23\\right)^2\\left(\\tfrac13\\right)\\times\\tfrac23=\\tfrac{8}{27}$. In five: $6\\left(\\tfrac23\\right)^2\\left(\\tfrac13\\right)^2\\times\\tfrac23=\\tfrac{16}{81}$. So $P(\\text{Ana wins})=\\tfrac{24+24+16}{81}=\\tfrac{64}{81}$, and the required probability is $\\dfrac{24/81}{64/81}=\\dfrac38$. The option $\\tfrac{8}{27}$ is the unconditional probability that Ana wins in four sets, $\\tfrac{10}{27}$ is the probability of four sets with either winner, and $\\tfrac45$ is the reverse conditional, $P(\\text{Ana wins}\\mid\\text{four sets})$.<\/p>", "tags": ["prob"], "rating": 8.8}, {"id": "EB1-26", "paper": "EB1", "n": 26, "q": "<p>Five friends each bring a wrapped present to a party and put it in a sack. The presents are thoroughly mixed, and the friends then take turns to take one present each, chosen at random from those remaining in the sack, until the sack is empty. What is the probability that at most one of the friends ends up with the present that they brought?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{7}{10}$", "$\\dfrac{89}{120}$", "$\\dfrac{2}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{19}{24}$", "$\\dfrac{11}{30}$", "$\\dfrac{3}{8}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>All $5!=120$ allocations are equally likely. Let $D_n$ be the number of ways in which none of $n$ people gets their own present. Listing gives $D_2=1$, $D_3=2$ and $D_4=9$. For five people, friend $1$ receives the present of one of the other four, say friend $2$ ($4$ choices). Either friend $2$ receives friend $1$'s present, and the other three are deranged in $D_3=2$ ways, or friend $2$ does not, which is like a derangement of four in $D_4=9$ ways. So $D_5=4(2+9)=44$. Exactly one person gets their own present in $5\\times D_4=45$ ways. The answer is $\\dfrac{44+45}{120}=\\dfrac{89}{120}$. The options $\\tfrac{11}{30}$ and $\\tfrac38$ are the cases of nobody and exactly one separately.<\/p>", "tags": ["prob"], "rating": 8.9}, {"id": "EB1-27", "paper": "EB1", "n": 27, "q": "<p>A glass paperweight is a solid right pyramid with a square base, and all eight of its edges have length $6\\text{ cm}$. A manufacturer wants to set the largest possible sphere entirely inside the paperweight. What is the radius of this sphere?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\text{ cm}$", "$(\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2})\\text{ cm}$", "$(6-3\\sqrt{2})\\text{ cm}$", "$\\dfrac{3\\sqrt{3}-3}{2}\\text{ cm}$", "$\\dfrac{3\\sqrt{6}-3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\text{ cm}$", "$\\dfrac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\text{ cm}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>By symmetry the sphere's centre is on the axis and the sphere touches the base and all four triangular faces. The half-diagonal of the base is $3\\sqrt2$, so the height is $\\sqrt{36-18}=3\\sqrt2$, and the height of each triangular face, from the apex to the midpoint of a base edge, is $\\sqrt{36-9}=3\\sqrt3$. Cut through the apex and the midpoints of two opposite base edges: the section is an isosceles triangle with base $6$, equal sides $3\\sqrt3$ and height $3\\sqrt2$, and the sphere appears as its inscribed circle. So $r=\\dfrac{\\text{area}}{\\text{semi-perimeter}}=\\dfrac{9\\sqrt2}{3+3\\sqrt3}=\\dfrac{3\\sqrt2}{1+\\sqrt3}=\\dfrac{3\\sqrt6-3\\sqrt2}{2}$. As a check, $r=\\dfrac{3V}{S}$ with $V=36\\sqrt2$ and $S=36+36\\sqrt3$. The section through two opposite vertices (sides $6,6,6\\sqrt2$) gives $6-3\\sqrt2$, but that circle would poke out through the faces; $\\tfrac{\\sqrt6}{2}$ is the answer for a regular tetrahedron of edge $6$.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 9.0}, {"id": "EB2-1", "paper": "EB2", "n": 1, "q": "<p>A sealed mineral sample contains two radioactive isotopes, $P$ and $Q$, neither of which is produced inside the sample. Isotope $P$ has a half-life of $1200$ years and isotope $Q$ has a half-life of $1800$ years. When the sample formed, it contained $8$ times as many atoms of $P$ as atoms of $Q$. Today it contains only one quarter as many atoms of $P$ as atoms of $Q$. How many years ago did the sample form?<\/p>", "opts": ["$18\\,000$", "$10\\,800$", "$6000$", "$9000$", "$3600$", "$3000$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>After $t$ years the numbers of atoms are proportional to $8\\cdot 2^{-t/1200}$ and $2^{-t/1800}$, so the ratio of $P$ to $Q$ is \\[8\\cdot 2^{-t/1200+t/1800}=8\\cdot 2^{-t/3600}.\\] The ratio itself halves every $3600$ years. Setting $8\\cdot 2^{-t/3600}=\\dfrac14$ gives $2^{t/3600}=32$, so $t=5\\times 3600=18\\,000$. Subtracting the half-lives ($600$ years per halving) gives $3000$, adding the decay exponents instead of subtracting them gives $3600$, following only one isotope gives $6000$ or $9000$, and reading the final ratio as $1:1$ gives $10\\,800$.<\/p>", "tags": ["explog"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "EB2-2", "paper": "EB2", "n": 2, "q": "<p>A rectangular frame has perimeter $40$ dm. To fit into its packing crate, the length of its diagonal must be at most $15$ dm, and for rigidity each of its sides must be at least $7$ dm long. Which of the following describes all the possible values of the area $A$ of the frame, in $\\text{dm}^2$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$87.5\\le A\\le 112.5$", "$91\\le A\\le 100$", "$87.5\\le A\\le 100$", "$49\\le A\\le 100$", "$0&lt;A\\le 100$", "$91\\le A\\le 112.5$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Let the sides be $a$ and $20-a$. The side condition gives $7\\le a\\le 13$. The diagonal condition is $a^2+(20-a)^2\\le 225$, that is $400-2A\\le 225$, or $A\\ge 87.5$. On $7\\le a\\le 13$ the area $A=a(20-a)=100-(a-10)^2$ takes every value from $91$ (at $a=7$ or $13$) to $100$ (the square, whose diagonal $10\\sqrt2$ is less than $15$), so the diagonal condition is automatically met and the answer is $91\\le A\\le 100$. Ignoring the side condition gives the lower bound $87.5$; turning the diagonal condition into an upper bound via $2A\\le a^2+b^2$ gives $112.5$; multiplying the two minimum sides gives $49$.<\/p>", "tags": ["alg"], "rating": 7.3}, {"id": "EB2-3", "paper": "EB2", "n": 3, "q": "<p>A ship sails in a straight line from a harbour $H$ on a course that makes an angle of $30^\\circ$ with the line from $H$ to a lighthouse $L$, where $HL=10$ km. The ship's radar can detect the lighthouse only when the ship is within $7$ km of it. The ship keeps to its course until it is far beyond the lighthouse. What is the total distance the ship sails while it is within $7$ km of the lighthouse?<\/p>", "opts": ["$4\\sqrt6$ km", "$\\sqrt{51}$ km", "$\\left(5\\sqrt3+2\\sqrt6\\right)$ km", "$5\\sqrt3$ km", "$10\\sqrt3$ km", "$2\\sqrt6$ km"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>The perpendicular distance from $L$ to the course is $10\\sin 30^\\circ=5$ km. The course cuts the circle of radius $7$ about $L$ in a chord of half-length $\\sqrt{49-25}=2\\sqrt6$, so the required distance is $4\\sqrt6$ km. Both ends of the chord are ahead of $H$, because the foot of the perpendicular is $10\\cos30^\\circ=5\\sqrt3\\approx 8.7$ km along the course, which exceeds $2\\sqrt6\\approx 4.9$. Equivalently, the cosine rule gives $s^2-10\\sqrt3\\,s+51=0$ for the distance $s$ sailed, and the roots differ by $\\sqrt{300-204}=\\sqrt{96}$. The sum of the roots, $10\\sqrt3$, the larger root $5\\sqrt3+2\\sqrt6$ and the half-chord $2\\sqrt6$ are the usual slips.<\/p>", "tags": ["trig"], "rating": 7.3}, {"id": "EB2-4", "paper": "EB2", "n": 4, "q": "<p>A walkway runs along the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=3$, with lengths in metres. Above it, the underside of a curved roof truss is modelled by \\[y=x^2-2kx+4,\\] where $k$ is a design constant. Safety rules require the truss to be strictly above the walkway at every point of it, that is, $y>0$ for every $x$ with $0\\le x\\le 3$. Which of the following describes all the values of $k$ that satisfy the rules?<\/p>", "opts": ["$k&lt;\\dfrac{13}{6}$", "$k&lt;2$", "$-2&lt;k&lt;\\dfrac{13}{6}$", "$0&lt;k&lt;2$", "$-2&lt;k&lt;2$", "$k\\le 2$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>At $x=0$ we have $y=4>0$ for every $k$. For $0&lt;x\\le 3$, the condition $x^2-2kx+4>0$ is equivalent to $2k&lt;x+\\dfrac4x$. On this interval $x+\\dfrac4x$ has least value $4$, at $x=2$ (by differentiation, or since $x+\\dfrac4x-4=\\dfrac{(x-2)^2}{x}$). So the rules hold exactly when $2k&lt;4$, that is $k&lt;2$. Checking only the ends of the walkway gives $k&lt;\\dfrac{13}{6}$, but $k=2.1$ fails at $x=2$. Demanding a negative discriminant gives $-2&lt;k&lt;2$, which wrongly rejects negative $k$: then the vertex is to the left of the walkway and the truss is fine. At $k=2$ the truss touches the walkway at $x=2$.<\/p>", "tags": ["alg"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "EB2-5", "paper": "EB2", "n": 5, "q": "<p>Two arithmetic progressions are described by their partial sums. For every positive integer $n$, the sum of the first $n$ terms of the first progression is $3n^2+4n$, and the sum of the first $n$ terms of the second progression is $5n^2-2n$. How many numbers less than $1000$ are terms of both progressions?<\/p>", "opts": ["$32$", "$33$", "$16$", "$1$", "$66$", "$34$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>The $n$th term of the first progression is $S_n-S_{n-1}=6n+1$ (and the first term $S_1=7$ fits), so its terms are $7,13,19,\\dots$, the numbers from $7$ upwards that leave remainder $1$ on division by $6$. Similarly the second has $n$th term $10n-7$, giving $3,13,23,\\dots$, the numbers from $3$ upwards ending in $3$. A number is in both exactly when it is odd, leaves remainder $1$ on division by $3$ and remainder $3$ on division by $5$, that is, when it leaves remainder $13$ on division by $30$. These are $13,43,\\dots,973$, which is $33$ numbers. Comparing only terms in the same position ($6n+1=10n-7$) finds just one common value, $13$.<\/p>", "tags": ["seq"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "EB2-6", "paper": "EB2", "n": 6, "q": "<p>A cargo ship is to make a journey of $300$ km at a constant speed of $v$ km/h, where harbour regulations require $0&lt;v\\le 15$. While the ship is moving, its running cost is \\[\\left(\\frac{v^3}{1000}+16\\right)\\text{ hundred pounds per hour.}\\] What is the least possible running cost of the whole journey, in hundreds of pounds?<\/p>", "opts": ["$400$", "$375$", "$320$", "$360$", "$387.5$", "$510$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>The journey takes $\\dfrac{300}{v}$ hours, so its cost is \\[J(v)=\\frac{300}{v}\\left(\\frac{v^3}{1000}+16\\right)=0.3v^2+\\frac{4800}{v}.\\] Then $J'(v)=0.6v-\\dfrac{4800}{v^2}$, which is zero only when $v^3=8000$, that is $v=20$, outside the permitted range. For $0&lt;v&lt;20$ we have $J'(v)&lt;0$, so $J$ is decreasing throughout $0&lt;v\\le 15$ and the least cost is $J(15)=67.5+320=387.5$. The unconstrained minimum $J(20)=360$ is the trap; $320$ counts only the fixed part of the cost.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff"], "rating": 7.5}, {"id": "EB2-7", "paper": "EB2", "n": 7, "q": "<p>The line $y=2x+c$, where $c$ is an integer, meets the circle \\[x^2+y^2-6x+4y-12=0\\] at two points $A$ and $B$. Given that the chord $AB$ has length at least $6$, what is the sum of all the possible values of $c$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$104$", "$-152$", "$136$", "$-136$", "$-104$", "$-120$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Completing the square, the circle has centre $(3,-2)$ and radius $5$. A chord of length at least $6$ has half-length at least $3$, so its distance $d$ from the centre satisfies $d\\le\\sqrt{25-9}=4$ (and then $d&lt;5$, so there really are two points). The distance from $(3,-2)$ to $2x-y+c=0$ is $\\dfrac{|6+2+c|}{\\sqrt5}$, so we need $|c+8|\\le\\sqrt{80}$. Since $8&lt;\\sqrt{80}&lt;9$, the integers are those with $|c+8|\\le 8$, namely $c=-16,-15,\\dots,0$, with sum $-136$. A sign slip in the distance formula gives $+136$; using $d\\le 3$ gives $-104$.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "EB2-8", "paper": "EB2", "n": 8, "q": "<p>A long horizontal pipe has a circular internal cross-section of radius $2$ m. Water flows along the pipe, and in every cross-section the water is $3$ m deep at its deepest point. Engineers define the <em>hydraulic radius<\/em> of the flow as the cross-sectional area of the water divided by the wetted perimeter, where the wetted perimeter is the length of the part of the pipe's internal boundary, in the cross-section, that is in contact with the water. What is the hydraulic radius, in metres?<\/p>", "opts": ["$1+\\dfrac{3\\sqrt3}{4\\pi}$", "$\\dfrac{8\\pi+3\\sqrt3}{8\\pi+6\\sqrt3}$", "$1-\\dfrac{3\\sqrt3}{4\\pi}$", "$1+\\dfrac{3\\sqrt3}{8\\pi}$", "$1+\\dfrac{3}{8\\pi}$", "$1$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>The water surface is $1$ m above the centre, so the dry part of the cross-section is a segment whose chord is $1$ m from the centre; it subtends an angle $\\theta$ with $\\cos\\dfrac{\\theta}{2}=\\dfrac12$, so $\\theta=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$. The dry segment has area $\\dfrac12\\cdot 4\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}-\\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\right)=\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}-\\sqrt3$, so the water has area $4\\pi-\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}+\\sqrt3=\\dfrac{8\\pi}{3}+\\sqrt3$. The wetted arc subtends $2\\pi-\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}=\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$, so it has length $\\dfrac{8\\pi}{3}$. The hydraulic radius is $\\dfrac{8\\pi/3+\\sqrt3}{8\\pi/3}=1+\\dfrac{3\\sqrt3}{8\\pi}$. Working with the minor segment as if the depth were $1$ m gives $1-\\dfrac{3\\sqrt3}{4\\pi}$; including the free surface in the perimeter gives the fraction with $6\\sqrt3$ in the denominator.<\/p>", "tags": ["trig"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "EB2-9", "paper": "EB2", "n": 9, "q": "<p>A circular running track passes through three marker posts with coordinates $A(1,3)$, $B(13,9)$ and $C(7,1)$, in units of $10$ m. A straight footpath runs from a gate at the origin $O$ and meets the track at a single point $T$, touching it there. What is the length $OT$, in the same units?<\/p>", "opts": ["$5$", "$5\\sqrt6$", "$10$", "$\\sqrt{10}$", "$10-5\\sqrt2$", "$5\\sqrt2$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>The perpendicular bisector of $AB$ passes through $(7,6)$ with gradient $-2$, and that of $AC$ passes through $(4,2)$ with gradient $3$; they meet at the centre $(6,8)$. The radius squared is $(1-6)^2+(3-8)^2=50$. Since $OT$ is tangent, the radius to $T$ is perpendicular to $OT$, so by Pythagoras $OT^2=\\left(6^2+8^2\\right)-50=50$ and $OT=5\\sqrt2$. (Equivalently, the track is $x^2+y^2-12x-16y+50=0$ and $OT^2$ is the constant term.) The distance to the centre is $10$, and the shortest distance from $O$ to the track is $10-5\\sqrt2$; adding instead of subtracting $r^2$ gives $5\\sqrt6$.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord"], "rating": 7.7}, {"id": "EB2-10", "paper": "EB2", "n": 10, "q": "<p>What is the largest integer that is less than $\\left(\\sqrt2+\\sqrt3\\right)^6$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$969$", "$968$", "$485$", "$970$", "$971$", "$484$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>First $\\left(\\sqrt2+\\sqrt3\\right)^2=5+2\\sqrt6$, and by the binomial expansion \\[\\left(5+2\\sqrt6\\right)^3=125+3\\cdot25\\cdot2\\sqrt6+3\\cdot5\\cdot24+48\\sqrt6=485+198\\sqrt6.\\] Likewise $\\left(5-2\\sqrt6\\right)^3=485-198\\sqrt6$, so the two cubes add to the integer $970$. Since $\\left(5-2\\sqrt6\\right)\\left(5+2\\sqrt6\\right)=1$, we have $0&lt;5-2\\sqrt6&lt;\\dfrac19$, so $\\left(5-2\\sqrt6\\right)^3$ is a tiny positive number and $\\left(\\sqrt2+\\sqrt3\\right)^6=970-\\left(5-2\\sqrt6\\right)^3$ lies just below $970$. The answer is $969$. Estimating with $\\sqrt6\\approx 2.45$ gives about $970.1$ and the wrong answer $970$.<\/p>", "tags": ["seq", "alg"], "rating": 7.7}, {"id": "EB2-11", "paper": "EB2", "n": 11, "q": "<p>Positive real numbers $x$ and $y$, with $x\\ne y$, satisfy \\[x^{y}=y^{x}\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad y^{2}=x^{3}.\\] What is the value of $x+y$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{45}{8}$", "$\\dfrac{81}{16}$", "$\\dfrac{27}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{20}{27}$", "$2$", "$\\dfrac{13}{4}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Since $y>0$, $y=x^{3/2}$. Taking logarithms of $x^y=y^x$ gives $y\\log x=x\\log y=\\dfrac32 x\\log x$, so \\[\\log x\\left(x^{3/2}-\\tfrac32x\\right)=0.\\] If $\\log x=0$ then $x=y=1$, which is excluded. Otherwise $x^{1/2}=\\dfrac32$, so $x=\\dfrac94$ and $y=\\left(\\dfrac32\\right)^3=\\dfrac{27}{8}$, giving $x+y=\\dfrac{45}{8}$. Check: both $x^y$ and $y^x$ equal $\\left(\\dfrac32\\right)^{27/4}$. Inverting the fraction when solving $x^{1/2}=\\dfrac32$ leads to $\\dfrac{20}{27}$, and forgetting the condition $x\\ne y$ suggests $2$.<\/p>", "tags": ["explog", "alg"], "rating": 7.7}, {"id": "EB2-12", "paper": "EB2", "n": 12, "q": "<p>A cup of coffee cools in a room whose temperature stays constant but is not known. Assume Newton's model: the excess of the coffee's temperature over the room temperature is multiplied by the same factor in any two time intervals of equal length. A thermometer in the coffee reads $92^\\circ\\text{C}$ at 09:00, $56^\\circ\\text{C}$ at 09:10 and $38^\\circ\\text{C}$ at 09:20. According to the model, at what time will the thermometer read $22.25^\\circ\\text{C}$?<\/p>", "opts": ["10:00", "10:10", "09:40", "09:45", "09:50", "09:30"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>If the room temperature is $R$, the excesses $92-R$, $56-R$, $38-R$ form a geometric progression, so $(56-R)^2=(92-R)(38-R)$. The $R^2$ terms cancel, leaving $3136-112R=3496-130R$, so $R=20$. The excesses are $72$, $36$, $18$: the excess halves every $10$ minutes. A reading of $22.25^\\circ\\text{C}$ is an excess of $2.25=\\dfrac{72}{32}$, which is five halvings after 09:00, so the time is 09:50. Assuming a room temperature of $0^\\circ\\text{C}$ gives nonsense; measuring five halvings from the 09:20 reading gives 10:10, and confusing the $30$ minutes after the last reading with a clock time gives 09:30.<\/p>", "tags": ["explog", "seq"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "EB2-13", "paper": "EB2", "n": 13, "q": "<p>How many values of $x$ in the interval $0\\le x&lt;2\\pi$ satisfy \\[9^{\\sin^2 x}+9^{\\cos^2 x}=4\\sqrt3\\,?\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$8$", "$4$", "$6$", "$0$", "$16$", "$2$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Let $a=9^{\\sin^2x}$. Because $\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x=1$, the second term is $9^{1-\\sin^2x}=\\dfrac9a$, so $a+\\dfrac9a=4\\sqrt3$, that is $a^2-4\\sqrt3\\,a+9=0$, giving $a=\\sqrt3$ or $a=3\\sqrt3$. These mean $\\sin^2x=\\dfrac14$ or $\\sin^2x=\\dfrac34$, so $\\sin x\\in\\left\\{\\pm\\dfrac12,\\pm\\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\right\\}$. Each of these four values of $\\sin x$ is taken exactly twice in $0\\le x&lt;2\\pi$, giving $8$ solutions. Keeping only one root of the quadratic gives $4$.<\/p>", "tags": ["trig", "explog"], "rating": 7.9}, {"id": "EB2-14", "paper": "EB2", "n": 14, "q": "<p>For which real values of $m$ does the equation \\[\\sqrt{x+4}=mx+2\\] have exactly two distinct real solutions?<\/p>", "opts": ["$0&lt;m&lt;\\dfrac12$ with $m\\ne\\dfrac14$", "$0&lt;m\\le\\dfrac12$ with $m\\ne\\dfrac14$", "$m\\ne0$ with $m\\ne\\dfrac14$", "$0&lt;m&lt;\\dfrac14$", "$\\dfrac14&lt;m\\le\\dfrac12$", "$m>0$ with $m\\ne\\dfrac14$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>The point $(0,2)$ lies on $y=\\sqrt{x+4}$, so $x=0$ is always a solution. Squaring gives $x+4=m^2x^2+4mx+4$, so $x\\left(m^2x+4m-1\\right)=0$. For $m=0$ only $x=0$ remains. For $m\\ne 0$ the other candidate is $x=\\dfrac{1-4m}{m^2}$, and it is a genuine solution exactly when $mx+2\\ge 0$ (then $x+4=(mx+2)^2\\ge0$ automatically). Here $mx+2=\\dfrac{1-2m}{m}$, which is non-negative exactly when $0&lt;m\\le\\dfrac12$. The candidate differs from $0$ unless $m=\\dfrac14$, when the line is the tangent at $(0,2)$. At $m=\\dfrac12$ the second solution is the end point $x=-4$, which counts. Accepting every root of the squared equation gives $m\\ne0,\\ \\dfrac14$; forgetting the end point gives the strict inequality.<\/p>", "tags": ["graphs", "alg"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "EB2-15", "paper": "EB2", "n": 15, "q": "<p>The boundary of a circular arena is the circle \\[x^2+y^2-4x+2y-20=0.\\] A floodlight stands at the point $P(4,1)$. Every straight chord of the boundary circle that passes through $P$ is considered. The midpoints of all these chords form a closed curve. What is the area enclosed by this curve?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\pi$", "$2\\pi$", "$4\\pi$", "$8\\pi$", "$25\\pi$", "$\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>The boundary has centre $C(2,-1)$ and radius $5$, and $CP=\\sqrt{4+4}=2\\sqrt2&lt;5$, so $P$ is inside. The line from the centre to the midpoint $M$ of a chord is perpendicular to the chord, so angle $CMP$ is a right angle (or $M$ is $C$ or $P$ itself). Hence $M$ lies on the circle with diameter $CP$, and every point of that circle arises from some chord through $P$. Its radius is $\\sqrt2$, so the area is $2\\pi$. Using $CP$ as the radius gives $8\\pi$.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "EB2-16", "paper": "EB2", "n": 16, "q": "<p>The volume $V$, in thousands of cubic metres, of water in a reservoir $t$ hours after midnight satisfies \\[\\frac{dV}{dt}=6\\sqrt t-t-8\\qquad\\text{for } 0\\le t\\le 25.\\] At midnight the reservoir holds $100$ thousand cubic metres. What is the least volume of water in the reservoir, in thousands of cubic metres, at any time in this $25$-hour period?<\/p>", "opts": ["$95.5$", "$88$", "$100$", "$87.5$", "$90$", "$92$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Integrating, $V=100+4t^{3/2}-\\dfrac{t^2}{2}-8t$. Writing $u=\\sqrt t$, the rate is $-(u^2-6u+8)=-(u-2)(u-4)$, so $V$ decreases until $t=4$, increases until $t=16$ and then decreases again. The candidates for the least value are the local minimum $V(4)=100+32-8-32=92$ and the end point $V(25)=100+500-312.5-200=87.5$. So the least volume is $87.5$, reached at the end of the period, not at the stationary point. (Also $V(16)=100$, so the greatest volume occurs twice.) Stopping at the stationary point gives $92$.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "int"], "rating": 8.1}, {"id": "EB2-17", "paper": "EB2", "n": 17, "q": "<p>Two vertical walls meet at a corner, making an angle of $60^\\circ$ with each other. Infinitely many circular discs lie flat on the floor in the corner. Each disc touches both walls; the first disc has radius $9$ cm, and each subsequent disc touches the previous one and lies between it and the corner. What is the total area of all the discs, in $\\text{cm}^2$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$81\\pi$", "$\\dfrac{729\\pi}{8}$", "$\\dfrac{729\\pi}{10}$", "$27\\pi$", "$108\\pi$", "$\\dfrac{243\\pi}{2}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>A disc of radius $r$ touching both walls has its centre on the bisector of the corner, at distance $\\dfrac{r}{\\sin30^\\circ}=2r$ from the corner. If the next disc has radius $s$, then the distance from the corner to the first centre is $2s+s+r$, so $2r=3s+r$ and $s=\\dfrac r3$. The areas form a geometric progression with first term $81\\pi$ and common ratio $\\dfrac19$, so the total is $\\dfrac{81\\pi}{1-1/9}=\\dfrac{729\\pi}{8}$. Using the radius ratio $\\dfrac13$ for the areas gives $\\dfrac{243\\pi}{2}$; taking the radii to halve gives $108\\pi$.<\/p>", "tags": ["seq", "trig"], "rating": 8.1}, {"id": "EB2-18", "paper": "EB2", "n": 18, "q": "<p>A taut drive belt passes around two pulleys of radii $9$ cm and $3$ cm, whose centres are $24$ cm apart. The belt crosses over itself between the pulleys: its two straight sections are both tangent to both pulleys, and they cross each other on the line joining the centres. Ignoring the thickness of the belt, what is its total length, in cm?<\/p>", "opts": ["$24\\sqrt3+12\\pi$", "$24\\sqrt3+8\\pi$", "$12\\sqrt3+16\\pi$", "$12\\sqrt{15}+16\\pi$", "$48+16\\pi$", "$24\\sqrt3+16\\pi$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>The crossing point $X$ divides the line of centres in the ratio of the radii, so it is $18$ cm from the larger centre and $6$ cm from the smaller. Each straight section makes an angle $\\alpha$ with the line of centres where $\\sin\\alpha=\\dfrac{9}{18}=\\dfrac12$, so $\\alpha=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$, and its length is $\\sqrt{18^2-9^2}+\\sqrt{6^2-3^2}=9\\sqrt3+3\\sqrt3=12\\sqrt3$. On each pulley the radii to the two points of tangency make an angle $2\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}-\\alpha\\right)=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ on the side facing $X$, so the belt wraps through $2\\pi-\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}=\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ on both pulleys. The arcs total $(9+3)\\cdot\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}=16\\pi$, and the length is $24\\sqrt3+16\\pi$. Assuming a half-turn on each pulley gives $12\\pi$ for the arcs; using the uncrossed tangents gives $12\\sqrt{15}$ for the straight parts.<\/p>", "tags": ["trig"], "rating": 8.1}, {"id": "EB2-19", "paper": "EB2", "n": 19, "q": "<p>A paper drinking cup in the shape of an open right circular cone is made by cutting a sector from a circular sheet of radius $6$ cm and joining the two straight edges of the sector exactly, without overlap. What sector angle, in radians, gives the cup of greatest capacity?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\pi$", "$\\pi\\sqrt2$", "$\\dfrac{\\pi\\sqrt6}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{2\\pi\\sqrt3}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{2\\pi\\sqrt6}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>The slant height of the cone is $6$, so its base radius $r$ and height $h$ satisfy $r^2+h^2=36$ and \\[V=\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\left(36-h^2\\right)h=\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\left(36h-h^3\\right).\\] Then $\\dfrac{dV}{dh}=\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\left(36-3h^2\\right)$, which is zero at $h=2\\sqrt3$, a maximum. There $r^2=24$, so $r=2\\sqrt6$. The arc of the sector becomes the base circumference: $6\\theta=2\\pi\\cdot 2\\sqrt6$, giving $\\theta=\\dfrac{2\\pi\\sqrt6}{3}$ (about $294^\\circ$). Swapping $r$ and $h$ gives $\\dfrac{2\\pi\\sqrt3}{3}$; assuming $r=h$ at the optimum gives $\\pi\\sqrt2$.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "trig"], "rating": 8.2}, {"id": "EB2-20", "paper": "EB2", "n": 20, "q": "<p>On a flat practice pitch, the two goalposts stand at points $A$ and $B$ that are $4$ m apart, and the goal line is the straight line through $A$ and $B$. For a point $P$ on the pitch side of the goal line (not on the line itself), the angle $APB$ is called the shooting angle at $P$. What is the area, in $\\text{m}^2$, of the region of the pitch from which the shooting angle is at least $45^\\circ$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$12\\pi+8$", "$8\\pi$", "$6\\pi+4$", "$6\\pi$", "$6\\pi-4$", "$2\\pi-4$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>On the pitch side, the points with angle $APB=45^\\circ$ form an arc of the circle through $A$ and $B$ in which $AB$ subtends $90^\\circ$ at the centre $O$; this circle has radius $\\dfrac{4}{\\sqrt2}=2\\sqrt2$, and $O$ is $2$ m from $AB$ on the pitch side. A point on the pitch side has shooting angle at least $45^\\circ$ exactly when it is inside or on this circle. So the region is the disc (area $8\\pi$) minus the minor segment beyond the goal line, whose area is $\\dfrac12\\cdot 8\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}-1\\right)=2\\pi-4$. The area is $8\\pi-(2\\pi-4)=6\\pi+4$. Taking the whole disc gives $8\\pi$, a sign slip with the segment gives $6\\pi-4$, and including the far side of the goal line doubles the answer.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord", "trig"], "rating": 8.2}, {"id": "EB2-21", "paper": "EB2", "n": 21, "q": "<p>Let $f(x)=x^{2/3}(x-5)$ for all real $x$, where $x^{2/3}$ means $\\left(\\sqrt[3]{x}\\right)^2$, using the real cube root. Which of the following statements are true?<\/p><ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$f$ has a local maximum at $x=0$.<\/li><li>$f$ is decreasing for every $x&lt;2$.<\/li><li>The equation $f(x)=-4$ has exactly three real solutions.<\/li><\/ol>", "opts": ["none of them", "II and III only", "III only", "I, II and III", "II only", "I and III only", "I and II only", "I only"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>For $x\\ne0$, $f(x)=x^{5/3}-5x^{2/3}$ gives \\[f'(x)=\\tfrac53x^{2/3}-\\tfrac{10}{3}x^{-1/3}=\\frac{5(x-2)}{3\\sqrt[3]{x}}.\\] For $x&lt;0$ both $x-2$ and $\\sqrt[3]x$ are negative, so $f'>0$ and $f$ is increasing; for $0&lt;x&lt;2$, $f'&lt;0$; for $x>2$, $f'>0$. So II is false. Near $x=0$ we have $x^{2/3}>0$ and $x-5&lt;0$, so $f(x)&lt;0=f(0)$: there is a local maximum at $0$ (a cusp, not a stationary point), and I is true. The local minimum is $f(2)=-3\\cdot2^{2/3}$, and $2^{2/3}>\\dfrac43$ because $4>\\dfrac{64}{27}$, so $f(2)&lt;-4$. As $f$ rises from $-\\infty$ to $0$ on $x&lt;0$, falls to $f(2)$, then rises without bound, the line $y=-4$ is crossed three times, so III is true. The trap is to look only for stationary points and miss the cusp.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "graphs"], "rating": 8.3}, {"id": "EB2-22", "paper": "EB2", "n": 22, "q": "<p>A window has the shape of the region bounded by the $x$-axis and the parabola $y=4-x^2$, with lengths in metres. A straight horizontal glazing bar is fitted along the line $y=h$, dividing the glass into two parts of equal area. What is the value of $h$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$2\\sqrt[3]{2}$", "$4-\\sqrt[3]{4}$", "$2$", "$4-2\\sqrt2$", "$4-\\sqrt[3]{2}$", "$4-2\\sqrt[3]{2}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>The part above the bar lies between $x=\\pm a$, where $a=\\sqrt{4-h}$, and has area \\[\\int_{-a}^{a}\\left(a^2-x^2\\right)dx=\\frac43a^3=\\frac43(4-h)^{3/2}.\\] With $h=0$ the whole window has area $\\dfrac{32}{3}$, so we need $\\dfrac43(4-h)^{3/2}=\\dfrac{16}{3}$, that is $(4-h)^{3/2}=4$, so $4-h=4^{2/3}=2\\sqrt[3]2$ and $h=4-2\\sqrt[3]2\\approx1.48$. The value $2\\sqrt[3]2$ is the depth of the bar below the top, not its height; assuming the area above the bar is proportional to its depth gives $h=2$, and assuming it is proportional to the square of the depth gives $4-2\\sqrt2$.<\/p>", "tags": ["int", "graphs"], "rating": 8.4}, {"id": "EB2-23", "paper": "EB2", "n": 23, "q": "<p>A cubic polynomial function $f$ has a local maximum at the point $(1,4)$ and a local minimum at the point $(3,0)$. Consider the following six equations.<\/p><ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$f(|x|)=1$<\/li><li>$|f(x)|=1$<\/li><li>$|f(|x|)|=3$<\/li><li>$f(|x|)=4$<\/li><li>$|f(x)|=4$<\/li><li>$f(|x|)=0$<\/li><\/ol><p>For how many of these equations is the number of distinct real solutions exactly four?<\/p>", "opts": ["$0$", "$6$", "$3$", "$2$", "$1$", "$5$", "$4$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>We have $f'(x)=k(x-1)(x-3)$, and $f(1)-f(3)=-\\displaystyle\\int_1^3k(x-1)(x-3)\\,dx=\\frac{4k}{3}=4$, so $k=3$, and with $f(3)=0$ this gives $f(x)=x^3-6x^2+9x=x(x-3)^2$. So $f\\ge0$ for $x\\ge0$; $f$ rises from $0$ to $4$ on $[0,1]$, falls to $0$ at $3$ and then rises, with $f(4)=4$. For $f(|x|)=c$, count the solutions with $x>0$, double them, and add $x=0$ if $c=0$. I: three positive solutions, so $6$. II: $f=1$ has three solutions and $f=-1$ has one (negative) solution, so $4$. III: $f(|x|)=-3$ is impossible and $f(|x|)=3$ gives $6$. IV: the positive solutions are $x=1$ and $x=4$, so $4$. V: $f=4$ at $x=1$ and $x=4$, and $f=-4$ once for $x&lt;0$, so $3$. VI: $x=0,\\pm3$, so $3$. Exactly two equations (II and IV) have four solutions.<\/p>", "tags": ["graphs"], "rating": 8.5}, {"id": "EB2-24", "paper": "EB2", "n": 24, "q": "<p>A cable hangs between the tops of two vertical poles that stand $4$ m apart on level ground. Taking the foot of one pole as the origin, the height of the cable above the ground at horizontal distance $x$ m is $f(x)$ m, where $f$ is known to be a polynomial of degree at most $3$. An assistant measured the heights at equally spaced points and applied the trapezium rule to estimate \\[\\int_0^4 f(x)\\,dx,\\] obtaining $20$ with $2$ strips and $18.5$ with $4$ strips, and then lost the measurements. What is the exact value of $\\displaystyle\\int_0^4 f(x)\\,dx$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$17$", "$17.75$", "$19.25$", "$18$", "$18.5$", "$18.25$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Write $T_2$ and $T_4$ for the two estimates and $I$ for the integral. All three depend linearly on $f$, so it is enough to compare them for $f=1,x,x^2,x^3$. For $1$ and $x$ the trapezium rule is exact. For $x^2$: $I=\\dfrac{64}{3}$, $T_2=24$, $T_4=22$; for $x^3$: $I=64$, $T_2=80$, $T_4=68$. In both cases $T_2-I=4\\left(T_4-I\\right)$: halving the strip width divides the error by $4$. Hence $I=\\dfrac{4T_4-T_2}{3}=\\dfrac{74-20}{3}=18$ (this is Simpson's rule). Such a cable exists, for example $f(x)=3.5+0.75(x-2)^2$. Taking $T_4$ as exact gives $18.5$; assuming the error halves gives $17.75$; assuming it is unchanged gives $17$.<\/p>", "tags": ["int"], "rating": 8.6}, {"id": "EB2-25", "paper": "EB2", "n": 25, "q": "<p>A particle moves along a straight line. At time $t$ seconds its velocity is $v$ m/s, where $v$ is a cubic polynomial in $t$ whose $t^3$ coefficient is $4$. The particle is at a point $O$ when $t=0$, and it is back at $O$ when $t=1$, when $t=2$ and when $t=3$. What is the greatest distance of the particle from $O$, in metres, during the interval $0\\le t\\le 3$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$2$", "$1$", "$\\dfrac12$", "$\\dfrac34$", "$\\dfrac{9}{16}$", "$\\dfrac{25}{16}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>The displacement $s$ from $O$ satisfies $\\dfrac{ds}{dt}=v$, so integrating, $s$ is a polynomial of degree $4$ with $t^4$ coefficient $1$. It vanishes at $t=0,1,2,3$, so \\[s=t(t-1)(t-2)(t-3)=\\left(t^2-3t\\right)\\left(t^2-3t+2\\right).\\] With $w=t^2-3t$, which runs over $-\\dfrac94\\le w\\le0$ for $0\\le t\\le3$, we get $s=w(w+2)=(w+1)^2-1$. This ranges from $-1$ (at $w=-1$, that is $t=\\dfrac{3\\pm\\sqrt5}{2}$) up to $\\dfrac{25}{16}-1=\\dfrac{9}{16}$ (at $w=-\\dfrac94$, that is $t=\\dfrac32$). The greatest distance is $1$ m. The symmetry about $t=\\dfrac32$ tempts many to stop at the local maximum $\\dfrac{9}{16}$, but the particle goes further on the negative side.<\/p>", "tags": ["int", "diff"], "rating": 8.7}, {"id": "EB2-26", "paper": "EB2", "n": 26, "q": "<p>The profile of a curved track is the curve \\[y=x^4-4x^3+3x^2+5x.\\] A straight rod is placed against the track so that it is tangent to the curve at two distinct points. What is the distance between these two points of contact?<\/p>", "opts": ["$2\\sqrt{15}$", "$2\\sqrt6$", "$\\sqrt{15}$", "$\\sqrt6$", "$3\\sqrt{10}$", "$\\sqrt{30}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>If the line $y=mx+c$ touches the curve at $x=p$ and $x=q$ with $p\\ne q$, then $x^4-4x^3+3x^2+(5-m)x-c$ has double roots at $p$ and $q$, so it equals $\\left(x^2-Sx+P\\right)^2$ with $S=p+q$, $P=pq$. Expanding, $\\left(x^2-Sx+P\\right)^2=x^4-2Sx^3+\\left(S^2+2P\\right)x^2-2SPx+P^2$. Comparing coefficients: $2S=4$, so $S=2$; $S^2+2P=3$, so $P=-\\dfrac12$; then $5-m=-2SP=2$, so $m=3$, and $c=-P^2=-\\dfrac14$. The points of contact satisfy $x^2-2x-\\dfrac12=0$, so they differ in $x$ by $\\sqrt{4+2}=\\sqrt6$, and since the rod has gradient $3$ their distance apart is $\\sqrt6\\cdot\\sqrt{1+3^2}=\\sqrt{60}=2\\sqrt{15}$. Stopping at the horizontal separation gives $\\sqrt6$.<\/p>", "tags": ["alg", "graphs"], "rating": 8.8}, {"id": "EB2-27", "paper": "EB2", "n": 27, "q": "<p>A tower is built from $10\\,000$ thin layers stacked one on top of another. For each $k=1,2,\\dots,10\\,000$, the $k$th layer from the bottom has thickness $\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt k}$ cm. What is the height of the tower, correct to the nearest centimetre?<\/p>", "opts": ["$201$ cm", "$198$ cm", "$100$ cm", "$197$ cm", "$200$ cm", "$199$ cm"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>The height is $H=\\displaystyle\\sum_{k=1}^{10000}k^{-1/2}$. Comparing rectangles with the area under $y=x^{-1/2}$ gives $2\\sqrt{10001}-2&lt;H&lt;1+\\displaystyle\\int_1^{10000}x^{-1/2}\\,dx=199$, so $198&lt;H&lt;199$; this fixes the integer part but not the rounding. Now apply the trapezium rule to $\\displaystyle\\int_1^{10000}x^{-1/2}\\,dx=198$ with unit strips: the estimate is $H-\\dfrac12\\left(1+\\dfrac{1}{100}\\right)=H-0.505$. Since $\\dfrac{d^2}{dx^2}x^{-1/2}=\\dfrac34x^{-5/2}>0$, the curve bends upwards and every trapezium lies above it, so the estimate exceeds the integral: $H-0.505>198$. Hence $198.505&lt;H&lt;199$ and the height is $199$ cm to the nearest centimetre (in fact $H\\approx198.54$). Quoting the integer part gives $198$, and using $2\\sqrt{10000}$ gives $200$.<\/p>", "tags": ["int", "seq"], "rating": 9.0}];
